const MCQS = [
  // Week 1 (1 - 22)
  {"num":1,"week":1,"q":"What is the main difference between a classical machine-learning pipeline and a deep-learning one?","A":"A deep network learns its own features from the raw input, while classical ML fits on features a human designed","B":"Deep learning needs less data and less compute, which is why it replaced classical ML","C":"Classical ML has no learnable parameters at all; only deep networks fit parameters to examples","D":"Classical ML learns its features from the raw input, while a deep network needs features designed for it first","ans":"A","exp":"Deep learning did not replace machine learning; it replaced the part where a human engineer decided what the useful features were. \"Classical ML learns its features...\" is the same sentence with the two pipelines swapped. \"Deep learning needs less data...\" inverts the trade-off: a deep network needs more data and far more compute. \"Classical ML has no learnable...\" confuses \"learned from data\" with \"has no parameters\" - an SVM fits parameters too."},
  {"num":2,"week":1,"q":"During a forward pass, in which order are the values produced?","A":"z, then the activation, then y-hat, then the loss","B":"The activation, then z, then the loss, then y-hat","C":"y-hat, then the loss, then z, then the activation","D":"The loss, then z, then the activation, then y-hat","ans":"A","exp":"Each neuron computes its pre-activation z first, passes it through the activation, and the last layer gives y-hat. Only once a prediction exists can the loss compare it with the target."},
  {"num":3,"week":1,"q":"In supervised learning, what do y and y-hat stand for?","A":"y is the true label and y-hat is the model's prediction","B":"y is the prediction and y-hat is the true label","C":"Both are predictions, taken at different layers","D":"y is the input and y-hat is the output","ans":"A","exp":"y-hat is always the model's output. y is the ground truth the loss compares it against. \"y is the input and...\" confuses the target with the input x."},
  {"num":4,"week":1,"q":"A single neuron has inputs x = (2, 3), weights w = (0.4, -0.2) and bias b = 0.1. What is the pre-activation z?","A":"0.1","B":"0.3","C":"0.5","D":"1.5","ans":"B","exp":"z = 0.4(2) + (-0.2)(3) + 0.1 = 0.8 - 0.6 + 0.1 = 0.3. Each input is multiplied by its own weight, the products are summed, and the bias is added once."},
  {"num":5,"week":1,"q":"Which of these is NOT something a loss function does?","A":"Decide how many neurons the hidden layer should have","B":"Define what counts as a mistake for this task","C":"Collapse a prediction and a target into one number","D":"Provide the gradient that the update rule follows","ans":"A","exp":"The loss scores an answer and supplies the gradient; the width of a layer is an architecture choice made by the engineer before training starts. \"Collapse a prediction and...\", \"Provide the gradient that...\" and \"Define what counts as...\" are all jobs the loss really does."},
  {"num":6,"week":1,"q":"A network is given one sample and produces y-hat = 2 when the target is y = 1. Using squared error, what is the loss?","A":"0.5","B":"1","C":"2","D":"3","ans":"B","exp":"L = (y-hat - y)^2 = (2 - 1)^2 = 1. \"0.5\" is the half-squared-error variant without stating it, \"2\" is the gap doubled, \"3\" adds the two numbers."},
  {"num":7,"week":1,"q":"Deep learning is a subset of machine learning. Which part of the classical machine-learning workflow does it take over?","A":"The step where parameters are fitted to the training examples","B":"The step where the task metric is chosen for the user","C":"The step where the data is collected and labelled","D":"The step where a human engineer decides which features are useful","ans":"D","exp":"Deep learning stacks layers so the model learns its own features, replacing the hand-designed feature step. \"The step where parameters are fitted to the training examples\" is wrong because classical learners such as SVMs and logistic regression already fit parameters. \"The step where the task metric is chosen for the user\" and \"The step where the data is collected and labelled\" are human framing and data work that a deep model does not remove."},
  {"num":8,"week":1,"q":"A model is Linear(4,16), then Linear(16,16), then Linear(16,3), with no activation function anywhere. What set of functions can it represent?","A":"Any continuous function, because three layers are stacked","B":"Only functions whose output is zero when the input is zero","C":"No function at all, because the layer widths do not match","D":"Exactly the functions a single Linear(4,3) layer can represent","ans":"D","exp":"A composition of linear maps is itself a linear map, so the three weight matrices fold into one 3-by-4 matrix plus a bias. \"Any continuous function, because three layers are stacked\" confuses layer count with expressive power, which only a non-linearity supplies. \"Only functions whose output is zero when the input is zero\" ignores the bias terms, which shift the output. \"No function at all, because the layer widths do not match\" is false: 4-16, 16-16 and 16-3 chain correctly."},
  {"num":9,"week":1,"q":"In a trained image network, what kind of structure do the later hidden layers represent compared with the early ones?","A":"Edges and oriented gradients, while the early layers hold object parts","B":"Raw intensity values, while the early layers hold class scores","C":"Single pixel values, while the early layers hold whole images","D":"Object parts such as wheels, eyes and letters, while the early layers hold edges and colour blobs","ans":"D","exp":"The hierarchy runs from edges and colour blobs, through textures and corners, to object parts, and finally to class scores. \"Edges and oriented gradients, while the early layers hold object parts\" inverts that order. \"Raw intensity values, while the early layers hold class scores\" puts the input and the output at the wrong ends. \"Single pixel values, while the early layers hold whole images\" describes no layer of the network."},
  {"num":10,"week":1,"q":"Which three ingredients does the course credit for making deep learning practical around 2012 rather than 1992?","A":"Bigger parameter counts, deeper stacks and longer training runs","B":"Interpretability tools, regulation and standard benchmarks","C":"Cloud storage, open-source licences and faster disks","D":"Data at scale, parallel compute on GPUs, and better training algorithms","ans":"D","exp":"ImageNet-scale labelled data, GPUs that made matrix multiplication cheap, and advances such as ReLU, dropout and better initialisation arrived together. \"Bigger parameter counts, deeper stacks and longer training runs\" are consequences of those ingredients, not the enabling causes. \"Cloud storage, open-source licences and faster disks\" and \"Interpretability tools, regulation and standard benchmarks\" were not the missing pieces that ended the earlier winters."},
  {"num":11,"week":1,"q":"In the five-stage deep-learning pipeline, what is settled at the Frame stage?","A":"The optimiser, the learning rate and the batch size","B":"The serving stack and the retraining schedule","C":"The architecture family and the layer widths","D":"The task, the input, the output, the metric that matters and the baseline to beat","ans":"D","exp":"Framing comes before any modelling: name the decision, define input and output precisely, choose the metric the user cares about and agree the baseline. \"The architecture family and the layer widths\" belong to the Model stage. \"The optimiser, the learning rate and the batch size\" belong to Train and tune. \"The serving stack and the retraining schedule\" belong to Deploy."},
  {"num":12,"week":1,"q":"What does the instruction to freeze the test set on day one mean in practice?","A":"Keep the test set fixed in size but re-sample its rows before every training run","B":"Evaluate on the test set after every epoch so the final number is stable","C":"Train on the test set last, once the training set has been exhausted","D":"Hold the test set aside at the start and evaluate on it once at the very end, tuning on validation data instead","ans":"D","exp":"A test set only measures generalisation while it stays unseen, so all intermediate choices are made on validation data. \"Evaluate on the test set after every epoch so the final number is stable\" turns the test set into a tuning set and inflates the result. \"Train on the test set last, once the training set has been exhausted\" destroys the held-out set entirely. \"Keep the test set fixed in size but re-sample its rows before every training run\" lets training rows leak into it."},
  {"num":13,"week":1,"q":"A hidden unit using ReLU has a negative pre-activation for a particular input. What does it pass to the next layer for that input?","A":"The pre-activation unchanged, since ReLU only rescales positive values","B":"The absolute value of the pre-activation","C":"A small negative value proportional to the pre-activation","D":"Nothing at all, because ReLU outputs zero for every negative pre-activation","ans":"D","exp":"ReLU keeps positive values and zeroes the rest, so a unit with a negative pre-activation is dead for that input and contributes nothing downstream. \"The absolute value of the pre-activation\" would make the unit fire hardest on the most negative inputs. \"A small negative value proportional to the pre-activation\" describes a leaky variant, not ReLU. \"The pre-activation unchanged, since ReLU only rescales positive values\" would leave the layer linear."},
  {"num":14,"week":1,"q":"A binary classifier ends with a sigmoid on its output pre-activation. What does that final sigmoid achieve?","A":"It removes the need for a bias term in the output layer","B":"It normalises the hidden activations so training is stable","C":"It forces the output to be either 0 or 1 so the class is decided","D":"It maps the real line into the interval (0, 1) so y-hat can be read as a probability","ans":"D","exp":"Sigmoid squashes any real pre-activation into (0, 1), which is what lets y-hat be interpreted as the probability of the positive class and compared with a threshold. \"It forces the output to be either 0 or 1 so the class is decided\" describes thresholding, which happens after the sigmoid. \"It normalises the hidden activations so training is stable\" describes normalisation layers. \"It removes the need for a bias term in the output layer\" is unrelated: the bias is still a learnable parameter."},
  {"num":15,"week":1,"q":"What does adding parameters to a network buy you?","A":"Capacity to generalise, because a larger model sees more of the input space","B":"Lower inference cost, because the work is spread over more weights","C":"A guarantee of lower training loss and lower validation loss together","D":"Capacity to fit the training data, not capacity to generalise to unseen data","ans":"D","exp":"Parameters buy capacity to fit; a network with more parameters than training examples can memorise the training set and remain useless on new data. \"Capacity to generalise, because a larger model sees more of the input space\" is the exact confusion the material warns against. \"A guarantee of lower training loss and lower validation loss together\" ignores that the two can move in opposite directions. \"Lower inference cost, because the work is spread over more weights\" has it backwards: more weights cost more per prediction."},
  {"num":16,"week":1,"q":"For which kind of problem does the course say a simpler classical model is usually the right first answer?","A":"Acoustic modelling for speech recognition from raw waveforms","B":"Image classification from raw pixels on a million labelled photographs","C":"Translation and summarisation of free text","D":"A few thousand rows of well-understood tabular data that must be auditable","ans":"D","exp":"On small tabular problems gradient-boosted trees usually match or beat deep nets, train in seconds and support auditing, so a deep model must earn its place. \"Image classification from raw pixels on a million labelled photographs\", \"Acoustic modelling for speech recognition from raw waveforms\" and \"Translation and summarisation of free text\" are all high-dimensional perception or language tasks where learned representations beat designed features."},
  {"num":17,"week":1,"q":"A fully connected layer maps 300 inputs to 40 units and every unit has a bias. How many learnable parameters does it hold?","A":"12,000","B":"12,040","C":"12,300","D":"12,340","ans":"B","exp":"The weight matrix is 40 by 300, giving 12,000 weights, plus one bias per unit: 12,000 + 40 = 12,040. The answer \"12,000\" drops the bias term, the most common slip in this calculation. The answer \"12,300\" adds one bias per input instead of per unit. The answer \"12,340\" adds both bias counts at once."},
  {"num":18,"week":1,"q":"A network is Linear(4,64), then ReLU, then Linear(64,3), with a bias on each linear layer. How many learnable parameters does it hold in total?","A":"448","B":"451","C":"515","D":"579","ans":"C","exp":"Layer one holds 4 x 64 + 64 = 320 and layer two holds 64 x 3 + 3 = 195, so the total is 515; ReLU holds no parameters. The answer \"448\" drops both sets of biases. The answer \"451\" counts only the three output biases. The answer \"579\" counts the 64 hidden biases twice."},
  {"num":19,"week":1,"q":"A trained model holds 300,000 parameters stored as float32. How much memory do the weights alone occupy?","A":"0.3 MB","B":"0.6 MB","C":"1.2 MB","D":"2.4 MB","ans":"C","exp":"One float32 parameter costs 4 bytes, so 300,000 x 4 = 1,200,000 bytes, which is 1.2 MB. The answer \"0.3 MB\" divides by 4 instead of multiplying. The answer \"0.6 MB\" assumes 2 bytes per parameter. The answer \"2.4 MB\" assumes 8 bytes per parameter."},
  {"num":20,"week":1,"q":"A hidden unit has inputs x = (2, 1), weights w = (0.5, -0.4) and bias b = 0.3, followed by a ReLU. What does the unit output?","A":"-0.9","B":"0.0","C":"0.6","D":"0.9","ans":"D","exp":"The pre-activation is 0.5(2) + (-0.4)(1) + 0.3 = 0.9, which is positive, so ReLU passes it through unchanged. The answer \"0.6\" drops the bias term. The answer \"0.0\" assumes ReLU zeroes a positive pre-activation. The answer \"-0.9\" flips the sign of the whole weighted sum."},
  {"num":21,"week":1,"q":"Two dense layers with no activation between them fold into one. The hidden biases are b1 = (0.4, -0.5), the output weights are W2 = (0.5, -0.2) and the output bias is b2 = 0.3. What is the equivalent single bias W2 b1 + b2?","A":"0.2","B":"0.3","C":"0.4","D":"0.6","ans":"D","exp":"W2 b1 + b2 = 0.5(0.4) + (-0.2)(-0.5) + 0.3 = 0.2 + 0.1 + 0.3 = 0.6. The answer \"0.4\" flips the sign of the second product. The answer \"0.3\" keeps b2 alone and forgets W2 b1. The answer \"0.2\" adds the hidden biases directly to b2 without weighting them by W2."},
  {"num":22,"week":1,"q":"A batch of 16 samples, each with 20 features, is passed through a single Linear(20, 6) layer. How many individual numbers does that layer output for the batch?","A":"6","B":"26","C":"96","D":"320","ans":"C","exp":"The output is one row of 6 values per sample, so 16 x 6 = 96 numbers. The answer \"6\" gives the width of a single row rather than the whole batch. The answer \"320\" is 16 x 20, the size of the input rather than the output. The answer \"26\" adds the two widths instead of using the output width."},

  // Week 2 (23 - 46)
  {"num":23,"week":2,"q":"A single perceptron is trained on XOR for a million epochs and still gets at least one of the four rows wrong. Why?","A":"No single straight line separates XOR's two classes, so the weights it is searching for do not exist","B":"The step function is not differentiable, so the perceptron rule cannot update anything","C":"The learning rate was too large, so the weights kept oscillating past the answer","D":"Four rows are not enough data; the rule needs more examples of each class","ans":"A","exp":"XOR's two 1s sit on opposite corners of the square, and the four rows force w1 + w2 + b to be both positive and negative at once. This is a fact about the geometry of the data, not a training failure. More epochs or more data cannot find something that is not there."},
  {"num":24,"week":2,"q":"What is the largest value the derivative of the sigmoid ever reaches, and where?","A":"0.25, at z = 0","B":"0.25, at z = 1","C":"1.0, at z = 0","D":"0.5, at z = 0","ans":"A","exp":"sigma(z)(1 - sigma(z)) is largest where sigma(z) = 0.5, which is z = 0, giving 0.5 x 0.5 = 0.25. \"0.5, at z = 0\" confuses the derivative with the activation value itself, which is 0.5 there."},
  {"num":25,"week":2,"q":"A hidden unit uses ReLU and its pre-activation comes out as z = -4. What does the unit output?","A":"-4","B":"-1","C":"0","D":"4","ans":"C","exp":"ReLU is max(0, z), so every negative pre-activation is clipped to 0. The answer \"4\" is the absolute value, which ReLU is not, and \"-4\" leaves the number untouched. A unit giving 0 has not failed - it has decided this input is not its business."},
  {"num":26,"week":2,"q":"Why does stacking two linear layers with no activation between them gain nothing?","A":"The composition of two linear maps is itself a single linear map","B":"The parameter count becomes too large to train","C":"The second layer always receives zeros","D":"The gradients cancel out during backpropagation","ans":"A","exp":"Multiplying by W2 after W1 is the same as multiplying by one matrix W2W1, so the pair can be replaced by a single layer. Depth only buys something once a non-linearity sits between the layers."},
  {"num":27,"week":2,"q":"A network has a 2-2-1 shape with a bias on every neuron. How many learnable parameters does it have?","A":"6","B":"7","C":"8","D":"9","ans":"D","exp":"Hidden layer: 2 neurons x 2 inputs = 4 weights, plus 2 biases = 6. Output: 2 weights plus 1 bias = 3. Total 9. Answers of 6 or 7 forget some or all of the biases."},
  {"num":28,"week":2,"q":"Sigmoid saturates for large |z|. What does that do to training?","A":"The derivative collapses towards zero, so weights feeding that unit barely update","B":"The forward pass slows down, but learning is unaffected","C":"The weights grow without bound","D":"The output becomes exactly 1, so the loss is undefined","ans":"A","exp":"Beyond about |z| = 5 the slope falls below 0.007, and every gradient travelling back through that unit is multiplied by it. The unit has saturated and has all but stopped learning. \"The output becomes exactly 1,...\" is wrong because sigmoid never actually reaches 1."},
  {"num":29,"week":2,"q":"A perceptron has w = (0.8, -0.3) and b = 0.2. For the input x = (2, 1), what is the pre-activation z = w . x + b?","A":"0.7","B":"1.3","C":"1.5","D":"2.1","ans":"C","exp":"z = 0.8x2 + (-0.3)x1 + 0.2 = 1.6 - 0.3 + 0.2 = 1.5. The answer \"1.3\" computes the weighted sum but drops the bias. The answer \"2.1\" flips the sign of the negative weight and adds 0.3 instead of subtracting it. The answer \"0.7\" treats both inputs as 1 and ignores that x1 = 2."},
  {"num":30,"week":2,"q":"A perceptron has w = (0.3, -0.1), b = 0.2 and learning rate 0.5. It is shown x = (1, 1) with target 0 and updates by w := w + lr*e*x, with e = target - prediction. What is w afterwards?","A":"(0.3, -0.1)","B":"(0.8, 0.4)","C":"(-0.2, -0.1)","D":"(-0.2, -0.6)","ans":"D","exp":"z = 0.3 - 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.4, so the neuron outputs 1 while the target is 0 and e = -1; each weight falls by lr*1*1 = 0.5. The answer \"(0.8, 0.4)\" adds 0.5 instead of subtracting it, which is the e = +1 case. The answer \"(0.3, -0.1)\" leaves w untouched, which is only correct when the prediction was already right. The answer \"(-0.2, -0.1)\" moves w1 alone, although both inputs are non-zero."},
  {"num":31,"week":2,"q":"A perceptron has w = (0.4, 0.2), b = -0.9 and learning rate 0.5. It is shown x = (0, 1) with target 1 and updates by w := w + lr*e*x and b := b + lr*e. What are w and b afterwards?","A":"w = (0.4, 0.7) and b = -0.9","B":"w = (0.9, 0.7) and b = -0.4","C":"w = (0.4, 0.2) and b = -0.4","D":"w = (0.4, 0.7) and b = -0.4","ans":"D","exp":"z = 0 + 0.2 - 0.9 = -0.7 gives prediction 0 against target 1, so e = +1; w2 rises by 0.5 and b rises by 0.5, while w1 is frozen because x1 = 0. The answer \"w = (0.9, 0.7) and b = -0.4\" moves w1 as well, ignoring that its own input was zero. The answer \"w = (0.4, 0.7) and b = -0.9\" updates the weight but forgets that the bias moves by lr*e on every correction. The answer \"w = (0.4, 0.2) and b = -0.4\" moves only the bias and leaves the weights unchanged."},
  {"num":32,"week":2,"q":"A hidden unit has w = (0.5, -1.5, 2) and b = 0.4 and uses a ReLU activation. For the input x = (2, 1, 0.5), what does the unit output?","A":"0","B":"0.5","C":"0.9","D":"3.9","ans":"C","exp":"z = 1 - 1.5 + 1 + 0.4 = 0.9, which is positive, so ReLU passes it through unchanged. The answer \"0\" assumes the pre-activation came out negative and was clipped. The answer \"0.5\" drops the bias before applying ReLU. The answer \"3.9\" flips the sign of the negative weight, adding 1.5 instead of subtracting it."},
  {"num":33,"week":2,"q":"An output neuron with w = (0.4, -1.2) and b = 0.1 receives the hidden vector h = (2, 0.5) and applies a sigmoid. What probability does it produce, to three decimal places?","A":"0.300","B":"0.550","C":"0.574","D":"0.818","ans":"C","exp":"The logit is 0.8 - 0.6 + 0.1 = 0.3 and sigmoid(0.3) = 1/(1 + e^-0.3) = 0.574. The answer \"0.300\" reports the logit itself as if it were already a probability. The answer \"0.550\" drops the bias, giving sigmoid(0.2). The answer \"0.818\" flips the sign of the negative weight, giving sigmoid(1.5)."},
  {"num":34,"week":2,"q":"A two-class output layer produces the logits (2.0, 1.0) and softmax is applied across the class axis. What probability is assigned to the first class, to three decimal places?","A":"0.269","B":"0.500","C":"0.667","D":"0.731","ans":"D","exp":"Softmax exponentiates first: e^2.0 = 7.389 and e^1.0 = 2.718 total 10.107, so the first class gets 7.389/10.107 = 0.731. The answer \"0.667\" normalises the raw logits as 2/(2+1) instead of exponentiating them. The answer \"0.269\" is the probability of the second class. The answer \"0.500\" assumes a one-point gap in the logits carries no information."},
  {"num":35,"week":2,"q":"How many learnable numbers does a single Linear(200, 40) layer hold, counting weights and biases together?","A":"8,000","B":"8,040","C":"8,200","D":"8,240","ans":"B","exp":"The weight matrix is 40 x 200 = 8,000 numbers and there is one bias per output unit, so 8,000 + 40 = 8,040. The answer \"8,000\" counts the weights only and drops the bias vector. The answer \"8,200\" attaches one bias to every input rather than to every output unit. The answer \"8,240\" adds a bias for each input and each output."},
  {"num":36,"week":2,"q":"A fully connected 4-6-3 network carries a bias on every unit of its two weight layers. How many learnable parameters does it hold in total?","A":"30","B":"42","C":"45","D":"51","ans":"D","exp":"The first layer holds 4x6 = 24 weights plus 6 biases and the second holds 6x3 = 18 weights plus 3 biases, so 30 + 21 = 51. The answer \"42\" counts 24 + 18 weights and drops every bias. The answer \"30\" stops after the first layer. The answer \"45\" gives biases to the output layer only."},
  {"num":37,"week":2,"q":"A 32 x 32 colour image with 3 channels is flattened and fed straight into a Linear layer of 256 units. Including biases, how many parameters does that layer hold?","A":"262,400","B":"786,432","C":"786,688","D":"789,504","ans":"C","exp":"Flattening gives 3x32x32 = 3,072 input features, so the layer holds 3,072x256 = 786,432 weights plus 256 biases, which is 786,688. The answer \"786,432\" drops the bias vector. The answer \"262,400\" uses 1,024 input features and forgets the 3 channels. The answer \"789,504\" adds one bias per input feature instead of one per output unit."},
  {"num":38,"week":2,"q":"A single perceptron is trained on the four rows of XOR. What is the highest accuracy it can reach on those four rows?","A":"50%","B":"75%","C":"92%","D":"100%","ans":"B","exp":"XOR is not linearly separable, so one line can satisfy at most three of the four rows, giving 3/4 = 75% as a hard ceiling. The answer \"100%\" would require a line that does not exist. The answer \"92%\" is the sort of figure that only appears when the labels, the data or the model are not what they are claimed to be. The answer \"50%\" is what a constant output achieves, and the perceptron can do better than that."},
  {"num":39,"week":2,"q":"Of the 16 Boolean functions of two variables, how many can a single perceptron represent?","A":"2","B":"8","C":"14","D":"16","ans":"C","exp":"Fourteen of the sixteen are linearly separable; only XOR and XNOR are out of reach of one line. The answer \"2\" is the number of exceptions, not the number that can be represented. The answer \"16\" would mean one neuron can compute XOR. The answer \"8\" assumes exactly half are separable, which the two-input case does not bear out."},
  {"num":40,"week":2,"q":"A hidden layer with W1 = [[1, 1], [1, 1]] and b1 = (0, -1) followed by ReLU is used to solve XOR. What does that layer do to the inputs (0, 1) and (1, 0)?","A":"It clips both of them to zero, so the output unit only ever sees (0, 0) and (1, 1)","B":"It maps them onto the same point as (1, 1), which the output unit then rescales","C":"It pushes them to opposite corners of hidden space so that the output line can pass between them","D":"It folds them onto the same hidden-space point, which one output line can then separate from the other two rows","ans":"D","exp":"Both rows give z1 = (1, 0) because the two rows of W1 are identical, so the two class-1 samples land on the single point h = (1, 0) and the line h1 - 2*h2 = 0.5 separates it from (0, 0) and (2, 1). The answer \"It pushes them to opposite corners of hidden space so that the output line can pass between them\" has it backwards: they are brought together, not separated. The answer \"It clips both of them to zero, so the output unit only ever sees (0, 0) and (1, 1)\" is wrong because h1 = 1 survives ReLU. The answer \"It maps them onto the same point as (1, 1), which the output unit then rescales\" would make the two classes indistinguishable, since (1, 1) maps to (2, 1)."},
  {"num":41,"week":2,"q":"Two Linear layers with nothing between them compute a = W2(W1 x + b1) + b2. Which single layer computes exactly the same function?","A":"A layer with weight matrix W2 + W1 and bias W2 b1 + b2","B":"A layer with weight matrix W1 W2 and bias b1 + b2","C":"No single layer can, because the two bias vectors cannot be merged","D":"A layer with weight matrix W2 W1 and bias W2 b1 + b2","ans":"D","exp":"Expanding gives (W2 W1) x + (W2 b1 + b2), so the composition of two affine maps is one affine map. The answer \"A layer with weight matrix W1 W2 and bias b1 + b2\" reverses the product order and leaves b1 outside W2. The answer \"A layer with weight matrix W2 + W1 and bias W2 b1 + b2\" adds the matrices instead of multiplying them, which is not even defined unless the shapes happen to match. The answer \"No single layer can, because the two bias vectors cannot be merged\" is wrong: W2 b1 + b2 is a single vector of the right length."},
  {"num":42,"week":2,"q":"Which activation is the recommended default inside hidden layers, and what is its main weakness?","A":"Sigmoid, because its outputs are restricted to the range (0, 1)","B":"Tanh, because it is not zero-centred","C":"Softmax, because it introduces one extra hyperparameter","D":"ReLU, because a unit whose input stays negative outputs zero and receives no gradient again","ans":"D","exp":"ReLU is the default hidden activation, and the weakness named against it is the dying unit: once its input stays negative it outputs zero and stops learning. The answer \"Sigmoid, because its outputs are restricted to the range (0, 1)\" misplaces sigmoid, whose real problem is saturation with a maximum slope of 0.25 and whose home is a binary output unit. The answer \"Tanh, because it is not zero-centred\" states the opposite of the truth, since being zero-centred is exactly what tanh has over sigmoid. The answer \"Softmax, because it introduces one extra hyperparameter\" confuses softmax, an output activation over a whole vector, with Leaky ReLU, which is the one carrying an extra hyperparameter."},
  {"num":43,"week":2,"q":"Which activation belongs on the output layer of a multi-class classifier, and what sets it apart from the others on the menu?","A":"ReLU, because it keeps the largest logit and zeroes the rest","B":"Tanh, because its range of (-1, 1) centres the class scores on zero","C":"Sigmoid, because it squashes each logit into (0, 1) one at a time","D":"Softmax, because it normalises across a whole vector so the outputs sum to 1","ans":"D","exp":"Softmax is the multi-class output activation, and unlike the others it is meaningful only over a vector: it divides by a shared denominator so the class probabilities sum to 1. The answer \"Sigmoid, because it squashes each logit into (0, 1) one at a time\" is the binary output choice and gives values that need not sum to 1. The answer \"ReLU, because it keeps the largest logit and zeroes the rest\" describes something ReLU does not do; it zeroes only negative values. The answer \"Tanh, because its range of (-1, 1) centres the class scores on zero\" produces negative scores, which cannot be read as probabilities."},
  {"num":44,"week":2,"q":"The slope of the sigmoid falls below 0.007 once |z| is beyond about 5. Why does that matter far more in a deep stack than in a single layer?","A":"A saturated unit outputs exactly 1, so every layer above it receives the same constant input","B":"Each layer adds its slope to the next, so the total slope grows until it overflows","C":"Evaluating one exponential per layer makes the forward pass of a deep stack far slower","D":"Those small slopes multiply layer by layer, so ten of them scale the signal reaching the first layer by less than 1e-20","ans":"D","exp":"The factors from successive layers are multiplied, so ten saturated sigmoids shrink the signal reaching the earliest layer by more than a factor of a million even before the weight matrices are counted. The answer \"Each layer adds its slope to the next, so the total slope grows until it overflows\" has the wrong operation and the wrong direction. The answer \"Evaluating one exponential per layer makes the forward pass of a deep stack far slower\" is about speed, not about the vanishing signal. The answer \"A saturated unit outputs exactly 1, so every layer above it receives the same constant input\" misreads saturation, whose range is open at 1 and whose cost is the lost slope, not a constant output."},
  {"num":45,"week":2,"q":"In a 784-256-128-10 network built from Linear layers with ReLU between them, how many learnable parameters do the ReLU layers hold?","A":"0","B":"256","C":"384","D":"235,146","ans":"A","exp":"ReLU is applied elementwise and stores nothing, so all of the parameters sit in the Linear layers. The answer \"384\" gives each of the 256 and 128 hidden units one parameter of its own. The answer \"235,146\" is the total for the whole network, every one of which belongs to a Linear layer. The answer \"256\" is the width of the first hidden layer, not a parameter count."},
  {"num":46,"week":2,"q":"PyTorch stores an nn.Linear weight tensor as (out_features, in_features). What shape is printed for the weight of Linear(128, 32)?","A":"(32, 32)","B":"(128, 32)","C":"(128, 128)","D":"(32, 128)","ans":"D","exp":"Linear(128, 32) takes 128 features in and returns 32, and because out_features is stored first the weight prints as (32, 128), which is why the manual forward pass needs x @ W.T. The answer \"(128, 32)\" reads the two arguments straight off the call and ignores the stored order. The answer \"(128, 128)\" squares the input width. The answer \"(32, 32)\" squares the output width."},

  // Week 3 (47 - 78)
  {"num":47,"week":3,"q":"How does mini-batch gradient descent differ from full-batch gradient descent?","A":"It computes the gradient on a small subset of samples and updates after each one, giving many updates per epoch","B":"It uses a different loss function, chosen so the gradient is cheaper to compute","C":"It uses every training sample but applies only one update at the end of training","D":"It uses exactly one sample per update, which is what makes it a mini-batch","ans":"A","exp":"Mini-batch descent takes B samples per step, typically 32 to 512, so 60,000 images at batch 128 give 469 updates in the epoch where full-batch gives 1. \"It uses every training sample...\" describes full-batch descent. \"It uses exactly one sample...\" describes pure stochastic descent, B = 1."},
  {"num":48,"week":3,"q":"A single weight sits directly on the output and the network is trained on squared error. For one sample the input is x = 2, the network answers y-hat = 7 and the true value is y = 4. What is the gradient of the loss with respect to that weight?","A":"-12","B":"3","C":"6","D":"12","ans":"D","exp":"The squared-error gradient at a weight is 2 x (y-hat - y) x x: the gap is 7 - 4 = 3, doubling gives 6, and multiplying by the input x = 2 gives 12. The answer \"3\" stops at the gap. \"6\" forgets the final multiplication by x - the input is what makes the same error charge different amounts to different weights. \"-12\" flips the sign and so sends the update uphill."},
  {"num":49,"week":3,"q":"Which is the correct gradient-descent update for a single weight?","A":"new w = old w - eta x dL/dw","B":"new w = old w - dL/dw / eta","C":"new w = eta x (old w - dL/dw)","D":"new w = old w + eta x dL/dw","ans":"A","exp":"The gradient points uphill, so the rule subtracts a step along it. \"new w = old w +...\" is the single most common error in this topic and sends the weights the wrong way; \"new w = old w - dL/dw...\" divides by the learning rate instead of multiplying."},
  {"num":50,"week":3,"q":"In a 2-2-1 network with ReLU hidden units, the input is x = (2, 1). Hidden unit h1 has weights (1, 1) and bias 0. What is h1?","A":"0","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4","ans":"C","exp":"z1 = 2(1) + 1(1) + 0 = 3, which is positive, so ReLU passes it through unchanged and h1 = 3. \"4\" is the answer for the other hidden unit, whose weight on x2 is 2."},
  {"num":51,"week":3,"q":"Continuing that network: h1 = 3 and h2 = 4 feed an output neuron with weights w5 = 2 on h1, w6 = -1 on h2 and bias 0, with no activation. What is y-hat?","A":"2","B":"6","C":"10","D":"14","ans":"A","exp":"y-hat = 3(2) + 4(-1) + 0 = 6 - 4 = 2. \"10\" is the commonest error: dropping the minus sign on w6 and computing 6 + 4 = 10."},
  {"num":52,"week":3,"q":"For the squared-error loss L = (y-hat - y)^2, what is dL/d(y-hat)?","A":"2 x (y-hat - y)","B":"(y-hat - y)^2","C":"2 x (y - y-hat)","D":"y-hat - y","ans":"A","exp":"Differentiating the square brings the exponent down as a factor of 2. \"y-hat - y\" is the derivative you get only if the loss carried a factor of 1/2. \"2 x (y - y-hat)\" has the subtraction the wrong way round and flips the sign of every gradient below it."},
  {"num":53,"week":3,"q":"In backpropagation, what is the local effect of a bias on its own pre-activation, that is d z / d b?","A":"1","B":"the input x","C":"the learning rate","D":"0, because the bias value is often zero","ans":"A","exp":"z = wx + b, so differentiating with respect to b gives 1 regardless of what the bias is set to. \"0, because the bias value...\" is the classic confusion between the bias VALUE being zero and its GRADIENT being zero; they are different things."},
  {"num":54,"week":3,"q":"Going backwards past an output weight w5 that sits on hidden activation h1, what does the running gradient get multiplied by?","A":"h1","B":"w5","C":"x1","D":"1","ans":"A","exp":"y-hat = h1 w5 + ..., so d y-hat / d w5 = h1: each weight is multiplied by the activation it carried. Note the reverse is also true and often confused - going back past w5 towards h1 multiplies by w5."},
  {"num":55,"week":3,"q":"A hidden unit uses ReLU and its pre-activation z was positive on this sample. What is dh/dz at that unit?","A":"1","B":"z","C":"h(1 - h)","D":"0","ans":"A","exp":"ReLU has slope 1 wherever z is positive and 0 wherever it is negative - an open or shut gate. \"z\" is the single most common slip: writing the value of z instead of the derivative. \"h(1 - h)\" is the sigmoid derivative, not ReLU."},
  {"num":56,"week":3,"q":"Backpropagating to weight w2 gives dL/dw2 = 4. With a learning rate of 0.1, what is the new value of w2 if it was 1?","A":"0.4","B":"0.6","C":"1.4","D":"1.6","ans":"B","exp":"new w2 = 1 - (0.1 x 4) = 1 - 0.4 = 0.6. \"1.4\" and \"1.6\" add instead of subtracting, which moves the weight uphill. \"0.4\" subtracts the gradient from the step rather than the weight."},
  {"num":57,"week":3,"q":"Why does the chain rule mean an early layer can receive a very small gradient in a deep network?","A":"The gradient reaching it is a product of many factors, and a product of small numbers shrinks fast","B":"The loss is computed at the output, so early layers are updated last and run out of time","C":"Early layers use a smaller learning rate by default","D":"Early layers have fewer parameters","ans":"A","exp":"Each layer contributes one factor to the product. Ten sigmoid layers at their very best contribute 0.25 each, and 0.25 to the tenth power is about one in a million. That is the vanishing-gradient problem."},
  {"num":58,"week":3,"q":"A regression model leaves residuals of -2, 1, 1 and 4 on four samples. Using L = (1/N) sum r^2, what is the mean squared error?","A":"2.0","B":"2.345","C":"5.5","D":"22.0","ans":"C","exp":"Square the residuals to get 4, 1, 1 and 16, add them to 22 and divide by N = 4, giving 5.5. The answer \"22.0\" is the squared-error total with the division by N forgotten. \"2.0\" is the mean absolute error of the very same residuals. \"2.345\" is the square root of the mean squared error, which is the RMSE and not the loss asked for."},
  {"num":59,"week":3,"q":"One faulty sensor reading pushes a four-sample batch to residuals of 0.5, -1.5, 2.0 and -4.0. What is the mean absolute error on that batch?","A":"-0.75","B":"2.0","C":"5.625","D":"8.0","ans":"B","exp":"MAE averages the magnitudes: (0.5 + 1.5 + 2.0 + 4.0) / 4 = 2.0. The answer \"-0.75\" averages the signed residuals, so the positive and negative ones cancel and almost all the error disappears. \"8.0\" is the sum of the magnitudes before dividing by N. \"5.625\" is the mean squared error of these residuals, which the single outlier dominates."},
  {"num":60,"week":3,"q":"Training minimises L = (1/N) sum r^2 over a batch of N = 5 samples. For one sample whose residual r is 3.0, what is d(loss)/d(prediction) for that sample?","A":"0.2","B":"1.2","C":"6.0","D":"9.0","ans":"B","exp":"The per-sample derivative of the mean squared error is 2r/N, so 2(3.0)/5 = 1.2. The answer \"6.0\" is 2r with the 1/N of the mean reduction dropped. \"0.2\" is sign(r)/N, which is what MAE would send back for this sample. \"9.0\" is r^2, the contribution this sample makes to the loss rather than its gradient."},
  {"num":61,"week":3,"q":"A batch of 8 samples is trained on mean absolute error, and one of those samples has residual -0.4. What does that sample send back as d(loss)/d(prediction)?","A":"-0.125","B":"-0.1","C":"-0.05","D":"0.125","ans":"A","exp":"The per-sample derivative of the mean absolute error is sign(r)/N = -1/8 = -0.125, and its magnitude does not depend on how large the residual is. The answer \"-0.1\" is 2r/N, the squared-error derivative. \"-0.05\" is r/N, which keeps the size of the residual instead of only its sign. \"0.125\" has the sign flipped and would push the prediction further away."},
  {"num":62,"week":3,"q":"A batch of 16 samples produces per-sample cross-entropy losses that sum to 12.8. With the default mean reduction, what scalar does the loss layer hand to backward()?","A":"0.8","B":"1.25","C":"12.8","D":"204.8","ans":"A","exp":"Mean reduction divides the summed per-sample losses by the batch size: 12.8 / 16 = 0.8. The answer \"12.8\" is what reduction set to sum returns, and switching to it silently multiplies the effective learning rate by the batch size. \"1.25\" divides the batch size by the loss instead of the other way round. \"204.8\" multiplies by 16 rather than dividing by it."},
  {"num":63,"week":3,"q":"Which pairing of a loss function with its task is correct?","A":"Mean absolute error for multi-class classification, because its gradient magnitude is constant","B":"Mean squared error for a single class label, because squaring makes any mistake expensive","C":"Categorical cross-entropy for an unbounded real-valued target, because its penalty is unbounded too","D":"Huber for regression, behaving like squared error near zero error and like absolute error far from it","ans":"D","exp":"Huber is a regression loss that is quadratic close to zero error and linear in the tails, which is why it carries an extra threshold to tune. \"Mean squared error for a single class label, because squaring makes any mistake expensive\" is the classic trap: behind a sigmoid, squared error mutes the gradient. \"Categorical cross-entropy for an unbounded real-valued target, because its penalty is unbounded too\" is undefined for such a target. \"Mean absolute error for multi-class classification, because its gradient magnitude is constant\" still measures distance in output space, not surprise in probability space."},
  {"num":64,"week":3,"q":"How does the gradient magnitude of mean absolute error compare with that of mean squared error?","A":"Mean absolute error grows faster than squared error, which is why outliers dominate an absolute-error fit","B":"Mean absolute error has zero gradient everywhere except at exactly zero error","C":"Both are proportional to the residual, so they differ only by a constant factor","D":"Mean absolute error gives a constant magnitude whatever the residual, while the squared-error gradient grows with the error","ans":"D","exp":"The derivative of |r| is sign(r), so every sample pulls with the same strength, while the derivative of r^2 is 2r, which grows with the error and lets one far-off point outweigh many good ones. \"Both are proportional to the residual, so they differ only by a constant factor\" is true of squared error alone. \"Mean absolute error grows faster than squared error, which is why outliers dominate an absolute-error fit\" reverses which loss chases the outlier. \"Mean absolute error has zero gradient everywhere except at exactly zero error\" describes accuracy, which is flat and therefore unusable as a loss; absolute error instead has a kink at zero."},
  {"num":65,"week":3,"q":"A binary classifier gives p = 0.25 for a sample whose true label is y = 1. What is the cross-entropy loss on that sample, to four decimal places?","A":"0.2877","B":"0.5625","C":"0.6021","D":"1.3863","ans":"D","exp":"With a one-hot target the loss reduces to the negative log of the probability given to the truth: -ln(0.25) = 1.3863. The answer \"0.2877\" is -ln(0.75), the loss the model would pay if the label were 0 instead. \"0.5625\" is the squared error (0.25 - 1)^2, which the slides note disagrees with cross-entropy by a large factor. \"0.6021\" uses log base 10 rather than the natural log."},
  {"num":66,"week":3,"q":"A sigmoid output gives p = 0.73 for a sample whose target is y = 1, trained with binary cross-entropy. What is dL/dz at the logit?","A":"-0.27","B":"-0.0532","C":"0.27","D":"0.73","ans":"A","exp":"With sigmoid and cross-entropy the sigmoid derivative cancels against the log, leaving predicted minus target: 0.73 - 1 = -0.27. The answer \"-0.0532\" multiplies that result by p(1 - p), which is the extra saturation factor squared error would keep. \"0.27\" reports y - p and has the sign the wrong way round. \"0.73\" is the prediction itself, not a gradient."},
  {"num":67,"week":3,"q":"A sigmoid output gives p = 0.1192 for a sample whose target is y = 1. Trained on squared error the logit gradient is (p - y) x p(1 - p). What is it, to four decimal places?","A":"-0.8808","B":"-0.1050","C":"-0.0925","D":"0.0925","ans":"C","exp":"p - y = -0.8808 and p(1 - p) = 0.1050, so the product is -0.0925, nearly ten times smaller than what cross-entropy sends back for the same prediction. The answer \"-0.8808\" is the cross-entropy gradient p - y, with no saturation factor applied. \"-0.1050\" is the factor p(1 - p) on its own. \"0.0925\" is the right magnitude pointing the wrong way."},
  {"num":68,"week":3,"q":"A three-class softmax gives p = [0.7, 0.2, 0.1] and the true class is the second one. Under categorical cross-entropy, what is dL/dz for the second logit?","A":"-0.8","B":"-0.2","C":"0.2","D":"0.7","ans":"A","exp":"dL/dz_k = p_k - y_k, and the true class carries y = 1, so 0.2 - 1 = -0.8. The answer \"0.2\" is p for that class with the target never subtracted. \"-0.2\" negates the probability instead of subtracting the target. \"0.7\" is the gradient at the first logit, where p = 0.7 and y = 0."},
  {"num":69,"week":3,"q":"Why does squared error behind a sigmoid output learn slowest exactly when the model is most confidently wrong?","A":"Because squaring a residual smaller than 1 shrinks it, so the loss itself is too small to act on","B":"Because the chain rule multiplies the error by p(1 - p), which is near zero when the output is saturated","C":"Because the sigmoid caps the loss at 1, so no gradient larger than 1 can ever be produced","D":"Because the optimiser reduces the learning rate on its own once the loss stops falling","ans":"B","exp":"Squared error keeps the sigmoid derivative p(1 - p) as a chain-rule factor, and that factor collapses towards zero at a saturated output, so the update is tiny at the worst possible moment; cross-entropy is built so the factor cancels. \"Because squaring a residual smaller than 1 shrinks it, so the loss itself is too small to act on\" confuses the loss value with its gradient. \"Because the sigmoid caps the loss at 1, so no gradient larger than 1 can ever be produced\" is wrong: the sigmoid bounds the output, not the loss. \"Because the optimiser reduces the learning rate on its own once the loss stops falling\" blames the optimiser for a property of the loss."},
  {"num":70,"week":3,"q":"An output layer computes z2 = W2 a1 + b2 with cached hidden activations a1 = [0.60, 0.30], and the gradient at the logit is dL/dz2 = -0.4207. What is the gradient of the second component of W2?","A":"-1.4023","B":"-0.4207","C":"-0.1262","D":"0.1262","ans":"C","exp":"A weight gradient is the running gradient times the local effect of that weight, which here is the cached activation it multiplied: -0.4207 x 0.30 = -0.1262. The answer \"-0.4207\" is the gradient of the bias b2, whose local effect is 1 rather than an activation. \"-1.4023\" divides by the activation instead of multiplying by it. \"0.1262\" is the right size with the sign reversed, which would turn the descent step upwards."},
  {"num":71,"week":3,"q":"Carrying dL/dz2 = -0.4207 back through W2 = [0.50, -0.60] into a hidden layer whose ReLU mask on this sample is [1, 1], what gradient arrives at the second hidden pre-activation?","A":"-0.2524","B":"-0.2103","C":"0.2103","D":"0.2524","ans":"D","exp":"Going back through a weight multiplies the running gradient by that weight, so -0.4207 x -0.60 = 0.2524, and a mask entry of 1 passes it through untouched; the sign flips because the weight is negative. The answer \"-0.2103\" belongs to the first hidden unit, -0.4207 x 0.50. \"-0.2524\" has the correct magnitude but keeps the incoming sign instead of flipping it. \"0.2103\" uses 0.50 and also flips the sign, mixing up both units."},
  {"num":72,"week":3,"q":"Along one path of a computational graph, dL/dp = -0.8, dp/dz = 0.21 and dz/dw = 2.0. What is dL/dw contributed along that path?","A":"-1.6","B":"-0.336","C":"-0.168","D":"0.336","ans":"B","exp":"The chain rule multiplies the local derivatives along the path: -0.8 x 0.21 x 2.0 = -0.336. The answer \"-1.6\" skips the activation factor 0.21 and is the sort of slip that makes a saturated unit look healthy. \"-0.168\" stops at dL/dz and never multiplies by the cached input 2.0. \"0.336\" is the same magnitude with the sign lost."},
  {"num":73,"week":3,"q":"A hidden layer has pre-activation gradient dL/dz = [-0.2103, 0.2524]. Which parameter gradient of that layer equals this vector exactly, and why?","A":"dL/dW, because each row of the weight matrix shares the pre-activation gradient of its own unit","B":"dL/db, because dz/db = 1, so a bias passes its pre-activation gradient through unchanged","C":"dL/dx for the layer input, because the gradient was computed with respect to what entered the layer","D":"dL/da for the layer output, because the activation is applied after the bias is added","ans":"B","exp":"In z = Wx + b the local derivative of z with respect to b is exactly 1, so multiplying the incoming gradient by it leaves the bias gradient equal to dL/dz, in every layer. \"dL/dW, because each row of the weight matrix shares the pre-activation gradient of its own unit\" misses the outer product: each weight gradient is that number times the cached input, so it is generally different. \"dL/dx for the layer input, because the gradient was computed with respect to what entered the layer\" requires another multiplication by W transpose. \"dL/da for the layer output, because the activation is applied after the bias is added\" sits on the other side of the activation derivative."},
  {"num":74,"week":3,"q":"In what order does a backward pass through a computational graph proceed?","A":"Compute every parameter gradient directly from the loss, so no cached intermediate values are needed","B":"Seed dL/dL = 1 at the loss, then at each node multiply the incoming gradient by the local derivative and hand it to the inputs of that node","C":"Apply the update rule layer by layer starting at the output, so each weight changes as its gradient arrives","D":"Start at the input with dL/dx = 1 and multiply local derivatives forwards until the loss is reached","ans":"B","exp":"The backward pass is seeded with dL/dL = 1 at the loss and then walks the graph in reverse, multiplying by one local derivative at a time and accumulating the result at the leaves. \"Start at the input with dL/dx = 1 and multiply local derivatives forwards until the loss is reached\" runs the sweep in the wrong direction. \"Apply the update rule layer by layer starting at the output, so each weight changes as its gradient arrives\" folds the optimiser into the sweep, which is a separate step. \"Compute every parameter gradient directly from the loss, so no cached intermediate values are needed\" throws away the forward cache that makes the backward pass cheap."},
  {"num":75,"week":3,"q":"Which statement correctly separates backpropagation from gradient descent?","A":"They are two names for one algorithm, which is why the learning rate is a parameter of backpropagation","B":"Backpropagation computes the gradients and stops there; gradient descent applies the update rule that moves the weights","C":"Backpropagation computes the loss value and gradient descent computes the gradients from it","D":"Backpropagation changes the weights layer by layer, and gradient descent only selects the learning rate","ans":"B","exp":"The backward sweep fills in a gradient for every parameter and changes nothing; the update rule w := w - eta dL/dw is what actually moves the weights, which is why an optimiser can be swapped without touching the gradient computation. \"Backpropagation changes the weights layer by layer, and gradient descent only selects the learning rate\" swaps the two jobs. \"They are two names for one algorithm, which is why the learning rate is a parameter of backpropagation\" is the belief that sends students hunting for eta inside the chain rule. \"Backpropagation computes the loss value and gradient descent computes the gradients from it\" credits the forward pass to backpropagation."},
  {"num":76,"week":3,"q":"A weight currently holding 0.50 has gradient dL/dw = -0.2524. After one gradient-descent step at eta = 0.50, what is its new value?","A":"0.1262","B":"0.3738","C":"0.6262","D":"0.7524","ans":"C","exp":"w := w - eta dL/dw = 0.50 - 0.50(-0.2524) = 0.6262, so a negative gradient makes descent increase the weight. The answer \"0.3738\" adds eta times the gradient instead of subtracting it, moving uphill. \"0.7524\" subtracts the whole gradient and ignores eta altogether. \"0.1262\" is only the size of the step, with the old weight never added back."},
  {"num":77,"week":3,"q":"A training set holds 50,000 samples and the loader hands out mini-batches of 250. How many parameter updates does one epoch perform?","A":"1","B":"200","C":"250","D":"50,000","ans":"B","exp":"Each mini-batch produces one gradient estimate and one update, so 50000 / 250 = 200 updates per epoch. The answer \"1\" is what full-batch descent gives, a single exact gradient for the whole pass. \"250\" quotes the batch size rather than the number of steps. \"50,000\" is one update per sample, which is pure stochastic descent at a batch size of 1."},
  {"num":78,"week":3,"q":"How does the noise in a mini-batch gradient estimate change as the batch size B grows?","A":"It is unaffected by B, which only changes memory use and the number of steps per epoch","B":"It falls roughly like 1 / sqrt(B), so the estimate stays unbiased but becomes steadier","C":"It falls roughly like 1 / B^2, so a batch of 32 is already effectively noise-free","D":"It grows with B, because more samples in a batch disagree with one another","ans":"B","exp":"A mini-batch gradient is an unbiased but noisy estimate of the full-batch gradient, and averaging more samples shrinks that noise by about 1 / sqrt(B), which is why doubling \"It falls roughly like 1 / sqrt(B),...\" is usually paired with a larger eta. \"It falls roughly like 1 / B^2, so a batch of 32 is already effectively noise-free\" overstates the gain badly. \"It grows with B, because more samples in a batch disagree with one another\" has averaging backwards. \"It is unaffected by B, which only changes memory use and the number of steps per epoch\" misses that the noise is exactly what the batch size controls."},

  // Week 4 (79 - 104)
  {"num":79,"week":4,"q":"What does momentum add to plain SGD?","A":"It averages recent gradients, so directions that persist build up and directions that flip sign cancel","B":"It clips the gradient whenever its norm exceeds a fixed threshold","C":"It gives every parameter its own learning rate, scaled by the size of that parameter's recent gradients","D":"It is exactly equivalent to multiplying the learning rate by 1/(1 - beta)","ans":"A","exp":"The velocity is an exponentially weighted average of recent gradients, so in a ravine the across-valley components cancel while the along-valley component accumulates. \"It is exactly equivalent to...\" is the common misunderstanding: a larger learning rate only multiplies the same bad direction, oscillation included. \"It gives every parameter its...\" describes the adaptive family, \"It clips the gradient whenever...\" describes gradient clipping."},
  {"num":80,"week":4,"q":"Which optimiser gives every parameter its own effective learning rate?","A":"Adam","B":"Gradient clipping","C":"Plain SGD","D":"SGD with momentum","ans":"A","exp":"Adam keeps a running estimate of the size of each parameter gradient and divides by it, so rarely-updated parameters take larger steps. Momentum changes the direction of the step but keeps one global learning rate for everything."},
  {"num":81,"week":4,"q":"A 10-layer network uses sigmoid everywhere and every pre-activation sits at z = 0, the most favourable case. Roughly what fraction of the gradient survives the trip back to layer 1?","A":"about 1 in a million","B":"none of it; it is exactly zero","C":"about 1 in 4","D":"about 1 in 100","ans":"A","exp":"Each layer contributes at most 0.25, and 0.25 to the tenth power is about 9.5e-7. It is tiny but never exactly zero, which is why \"none of it; it is...\" is wrong and why the problem is called vanishing rather than disappearing."},
  {"num":82,"week":4,"q":"Why does raising the learning rate NOT fix vanishing gradients?","A":"The learning rate is global, so waking the early layers blows up the late ones","B":"The learning rate has no effect on the size of any step","C":"Vanishing gradients only happen when the learning rate is already too high","D":"It does fix them, and is the standard remedy","ans":"A","exp":"Multiplying the rate by a million to move layer 1 multiplies layer 10 by a million too, and that layer was training perfectly well. The fix is in the activation functions and the initialisation, not the step size."},
  {"num":83,"week":4,"q":"What is the purpose of He initialisation?","A":"To choose the weight variance so the signal neither grows nor shrinks as it passes through layers","B":"To make the first epoch run faster","C":"To guarantee the network cannot overfit","D":"To set every weight to zero so training starts from a clean slate","ans":"A","exp":"He picks Var(w) = 2/n_in, the 2 compensating for ReLU zeroing half its inputs. \"To set every weight to...\" is explicitly the one scheme never to use: identical weights make every unit in a layer learn exactly the same thing."},
  {"num":84,"week":4,"q":"What problem does gradient clipping address?","A":"Gradients that explode to very large values","B":"Gradients that are computed in the wrong order","C":"Learning rates that decay too quickly","D":"Gradients that vanish towards zero","ans":"A","exp":"Clipping rescales the gradient whenever its norm passes a threshold, which is a seatbelt against a single huge step wrecking the weights. It does nothing at all for vanishing gradients, which are too small rather than too large."},
  {"num":85,"week":4,"q":"A ravine in the loss surface is steep across a narrow valley and nearly flat along it. What does plain SGD do there?","A":"It zig-zags across the valley instead of travelling along it","B":"It stops immediately, because the gradient is zero","C":"It diverges to infinity regardless of the learning rate","D":"It travels smoothly down the valley floor","ans":"A","exp":"The across-valley gradient is much larger than the along-valley one, so each step is dominated by the direction you do not want. This is precisely the situation momentum was designed to fix."},
  {"num":86,"week":4,"q":"Two optimisers are run on the same problem. One keeps a single global learning rate and adds a velocity term; the other divides each step by a running estimate of that parameter gradient size. Which pair is this?","A":"Momentum and RMSProp","B":"RMSProp and momentum","C":"Adam and momentum","D":"SGD and momentum","ans":"A","exp":"The velocity term is momentum; dividing by a running estimate of gradient magnitude is RMSProp, which Adam then combines with momentum. \"RMSProp and momentum\" has the two descriptions swapped."},
  {"num":87,"week":4,"q":"Momentum in the averaging form v := 0.9v + 0.1g starts from v = 0, and backprop returns g1 = 0.80 then g2 = 0.50. What is v after the second update?","A":"0.122","B":"0.130","C":"0.650","D":"1.220","ans":"A","exp":"v1 = 0.1 x 0.80 = 0.080, then v2 = 0.9 x 0.080 + 0.1 x 0.50 = 0.072 + 0.050 = 0.122. The answer \"0.130\" forgets to multiply the old velocity by 0.9 and just adds the two scaled gradients. The answer \"0.650\" is the plain mean of 0.80 and 0.50, which is not an exponential average. The answer \"1.220\" is the heavy-ball form v := 0.9v + g, which omits the (1 - beta) factor."},
  {"num":88,"week":4,"q":"PyTorch heavy-ball momentum uses v := 0.9v + g and w := w - eta*v, with v = 0, w = 1.000 and eta = 0.01. The gradients are g1 = 0.50 then g2 = 0.30. What is w after two updates?","A":"0.87500","B":"0.98750","C":"0.99200","D":"0.99875","ans":"B","exp":"v1 = 0.50 so w = 1.000 - 0.01 x 0.50 = 0.995, then v2 = 0.9 x 0.50 + 0.30 = 0.75 so w = 0.995 - 0.01 x 0.75 = 0.98750. The answer \"0.99200\" is plain SGD on the same two gradients, with no velocity at all. The answer \"0.99875\" is the averaging form v := 0.9v + 0.1g, whose steps are ten times smaller. The answer \"0.87500\" multiplies eta by ten as well as using the heavy-ball form, which double-counts the 1/(1 - beta) factor."},
  {"num":89,"week":4,"q":"RMSProp without bias correction uses v := 0.9v + 0.1g^2 and w := w - eta*g/(sqrt(v) + eps), starting from v = 0 with eta = 0.02. How large is the very first step?","A":"0.0063","B":"0.0200","C":"0.0632","D":"0.2000","ans":"C","exp":"At t = 1, v = 0.1g^2 so sqrt(v) = g x sqrt(0.1); the g cancels and the step is eta x sqrt(10) = 0.02 x 3.1623 = 0.0632, whatever the gradient was. The answer \"0.0200\" is eta itself, which is Adam first step, not RMSProp. The answer \"0.2000\" multiplies eta by ten instead of by sqrt(10). The answer \"0.0063\" divides eta by sqrt(10) rather than multiplying, which is the sign of the bias the wrong way round."},
  {"num":90,"week":4,"q":"Adam corrects its first moment by dividing m by 1 - b1^t, with b1 = 0.9. What is that divisor at t = 3?","A":"0.100","B":"0.271","C":"0.729","D":"0.900","ans":"B","exp":"1 - 0.9^3 = 1 - 0.729 = 0.271, so the raw m is scaled up by about 3.7 times at the third step. The answer \"0.729\" is 0.9^3 itself, the term that has to be subtracted from one. The answer \"0.100\" is the t = 1 divisor, 1 - 0.9. The answer \"0.900\" is b1, which is not a bias-correction divisor at any step."},
  {"num":91,"week":4,"q":"A 15-layer network has a typical per-layer Jacobian factor of 0.50. What fraction of the output-layer gradient reaches layer 1?","A":"3.1e-05","B":"6.1e-05","C":"9.8e-04","D":"7.5e+00","ans":"A","exp":"0.50^15 = 3.1e-05, because the backward pass multiplies fifteen such factors together. The answer \"6.1e-05\" is 0.50^14, one factor short. The answer \"9.8e-04\" is 0.50^10, the ten-layer figure rather than the fifteen-layer one. The answer \"7.5e+00\" multiplies 0.50 by 15 instead of raising it to the fifteenth power, treating a product as a sum."},
  {"num":92,"week":4,"q":"Weights are set too large, giving a 20-layer network a typical per-layer Jacobian factor of 1.30. By roughly what factor is the output-layer gradient multiplied by the time it reaches layer 1?","A":"26","B":"38","C":"51","D":"190","ans":"D","exp":"1.30^20 is about 190, so one update tears the early weights apart. The answer \"26\" multiplies 1.30 by 20 rather than raising it to the twentieth power. The answer \"38\" is 1.20^20, the right depth with the wrong per-layer factor. The answer \"51\" is 1.30^15, the right factor with the wrong depth."},
  {"num":93,"week":4,"q":"He initialisation draws weights from a normal distribution with variance 2/n_in. For a layer with 512 inputs, what standard deviation does that give?","A":"0.0039","B":"0.0442","C":"0.0625","D":"0.0884","ans":"C","exp":"sqrt(2/512) = sqrt(0.00390625) = 0.0625. The answer \"0.0039\" is the variance 2/512, not its square root. The answer \"0.0442\" is sqrt(1/512), which drops the factor of two that ReLU discarded half demands. The answer \"0.0884\" is sqrt(2/256), the correct formula with the wrong fan-in."},
  {"num":94,"week":4,"q":"Xavier initialisation uses weight variance 2/(n_in + n_out). A tanh layer has 100 inputs and 300 outputs. What standard deviation does Xavier prescribe?","A":"0.0050","B":"0.0707","C":"0.0816","D":"0.1414","ans":"B","exp":"sqrt(2/(100 + 300)) = sqrt(0.005) = 0.0707, which balances the forward and the backward condition. The answer \"0.0050\" is the variance 2/400 left unrooted. The answer \"0.1414\" is sqrt(2/100), which is He on the fan-in and ignores n_out entirely. The answer \"0.0816\" is sqrt(2/300), using the fan-out alone instead of the sum."},
  {"num":95,"week":4,"q":"Cosine annealing sets eta_t = eta_min + 0.5*(eta_max - eta_min)*(1 + cos(pi*t/T)), with eta_max = 4e-3, eta_min = 0 and T = 200 epochs. What is the learning rate at t = 50?","A":"5.86e-04","B":"2.00e-03","C":"3.41e-03","D":"4.00e-03","ans":"C","exp":"cos(pi x 50/200) = cos(pi/4) = 0.7071, so eta = 0.5 x 4e-3 x 1.7071 = 3.41e-03; the opening quarter of the run is deliberately flat. The answer \"2.00e-03\" is half the peak, which is the value at the halfway point t = 100, not at t = 50. The answer \"5.86e-04\" is the value at t = 150, the mirror point in the second half. The answer \"4.00e-03\" is the peak itself, which only holds at t = 0."},
  {"num":96,"week":4,"q":"A 40,000-step run ramps the learning rate linearly from 0 to eta_max = 1e-3 over the first 5 percent of its steps. What is the learning rate at step 500?","A":"1.25e-05","B":"5.00e-05","C":"2.50e-04","D":"1.00e-03","ans":"C","exp":"Warm-up lasts 0.05 x 40,000 = 2,000 steps, and step 500 is a quarter of the way through it, so eta = 0.25 x 1e-3 = 2.50e-04. The answer \"1.25e-05\" ramps over all 40,000 steps instead of over the warm-up window. The answer \"5.00e-05\" takes 5 percent of eta_max rather than 5 percent of the steps. The answer \"1.00e-03\" is the target rate, which is only reached at the end of the ramp."},
  {"num":97,"week":4,"q":"clip_grad_norm_ is called with max_norm = 1.0 and the measured total gradient norm is 12.5. By what factor is every component of the gradient multiplied?","A":"0.08","B":"0.92","C":"1.00","D":"12.50","ans":"A","exp":"The whole vector is scaled by max_norm/total = 1.0/12.5 = 0.08, which brings its norm down to exactly 1.0. The answer \"12.50\" inverts the ratio and would make the explosion worse. The answer \"0.92\" is 1 - 0.08, as if the excess were subtracted instead of the vector being rescaled. The answer \"1.00\" is max_norm itself, which is the resulting norm rather than the scale factor."},
  {"num":98,"week":4,"q":"RMSProp keeps AdaGrad idea of dividing by a root of squared gradients but changes one thing. What does it change?","A":"It divides the step by the sum of all past gradients rather than by their squares","B":"It replaces the ever-growing sum of past squared gradients with an exponential moving average","C":"It adds a momentum term on the gradient itself, which AdaGrad does not have","D":"It clips each squared gradient at a fixed threshold before accumulating it","ans":"B","exp":"AdaGrad sum only grows, so the effective rate decays towards zero and long runs stall; RMSProp uses an exponential moving average instead, so the scale tracks recent magnitude. The answer \"It adds a momentum term on the gradient itself\" is wrong because RMSProp has no momentum by default, which is exactly what Adam adds. The answer \"It divides the step by the sum of all past gradients\" drops the squaring that makes the quantity a magnitude. The answer \"It clips each squared gradient at a fixed threshold\" confuses adaptive scaling with gradient clipping."},
  {"num":99,"week":4,"q":"Adam divides m by 1 - b1^t and v by 1 - b2^t in its opening steps. What does this correction fix?","A":"It stops the denominator from reaching zero, which eps on its own cannot do","B":"m and v both start at zero, so the raw estimates are biased towards zero and the first steps would be far too small","C":"It applies weight decay outside the adaptive scaling, as AdamW does","D":"It keeps the learning rate from decaying towards zero over a long run","ans":"B","exp":"Both moments are initialised at zero, so early averages understate the true mean and mean square; dividing by 1 - beta^t removes that bias exactly and makes the first step equal eta. The answer \"It keeps the learning rate from decaying towards zero\" describes what RMSProp fixes in AdaGrad, not what bias correction does. The answer \"It stops the denominator from reaching zero\" is the job of eps. The answer \"It applies weight decay outside the adaptive scaling\" is the AdamW change, which is unrelated to bias correction."},
  {"num":100,"week":4,"q":"Input features on very different scales make the loss surface ill-conditioned. Why does that hurt gradient descent with one global learning rate?","A":"The loss stops being differentiable, so no update can be formed at all","B":"Some directions are hundreds of times steeper, so the single step size is set by the steepest one and is far too small for the rest","C":"The gradient becomes exactly zero in every direction at the same time","D":"Backpropagation can no longer compute the partial derivatives correctly","ans":"B","exp":"With badly scaled features the curvature differs enormously between directions, and one step size has to be safe for the steepest direction, which leaves it wasted on all the others. The answer \"The gradient becomes exactly zero in every direction\" describes a saddle point or a plateau instead. The answer \"Backpropagation can no longer compute the partial derivatives correctly\" is wrong because the chain rule is exact regardless of scaling. The answer \"The loss stops being differentiable\" confuses conditioning with smoothness."},
  {"num":101,"week":4,"q":"What makes the gradient arriving at an early layer shrink or grow geometrically as a network gets deeper?","A":"The loss is averaged over the mini-batch, so the gradient is divided by the batch size once per layer","B":"It is a product of one Jacobian factor per layer, so factors below one shrink it and factors above one blow it up","C":"Each layer adds its own gradient to a running total, so the sum grows linearly with depth","D":"Early layers hold fewer parameters than later ones, so their gradients are smaller by construction","ans":"B","exp":"The chain rule makes dL/dh_0 the product of L factors of the form W^T diag(f-prime(z)), and a repeated multiplication is a power law, so a typical factor of 0.25 or of 1.2 decides everything. The answer \"Each layer adds its own gradient to a running total\" would give linear, not geometric, behaviour. The answer \"The loss is averaged over the mini-batch\" applies once, not once per layer. The answer \"Early layers hold fewer parameters than later ones\" is not generally true and would not produce a power law anyway."},
  {"num":102,"week":4,"q":"Why is setting every weight in a layer to zero never used as an initialisation?","A":"The optimiser would have to divide by a zero gradient, so the first step crashes","B":"Every unit in the layer then computes the same output and receives the same gradient, so they never learn anything different","C":"It makes the pre-activation standard deviation 16, which saturates a sigmoid instantly","D":"Zero weights leave the loss undefined on the first forward pass","ans":"B","exp":"Identical weights make identical units, and identical units get identical gradients for ever, so the layer behaves like a single unit no matter how wide it is. The answer \"Zero weights leave the loss undefined on the first forward pass\" is wrong because the forward pass runs perfectly well. The answer \"The optimiser would have to divide by a zero gradient\" is wrong because no optimiser here divides by the gradient. The answer \"It makes the pre-activation standard deviation 16\" describes drawing weights from N(0, 1) with 256 inputs, which is the opposite mistake."},
  {"num":103,"week":4,"q":"clip_grad_norm_ rescales the gradient when its total norm exceeds max_norm. What does the rescaling leave unchanged?","A":"The number of parameters that get an update, because components below the threshold are set to zero","B":"The direction of the gradient vector, because every component is multiplied by the same factor","C":"The length of the gradient vector, because only the signs of the components change","D":"The largest single component, because only the smaller components are reduced","ans":"B","exp":"Clipping by norm multiplies the whole vector by one scalar, so the step still points downhill and only its length changes. The answer \"The length of the gradient vector\" is backwards: the length is precisely what clipping changes. The answer \"The largest single component\" describes clipping by value, which treats components separately. The answer \"The number of parameters that get an update\" is wrong because nothing is zeroed, so every parameter still moves."},
  {"num":104,"week":4,"q":"Giving each layer its own learning rate is sometimes proposed as a cure for vanishing gradients. Why is it still not a fix?","A":"Per-layer learning rates cannot be expressed in torch.optim at all","B":"What arrives at an early layer is not merely small but dominated by numerical noise, so scaling it up only amplifies the noise","C":"Each layer would then need its own loss function, which backpropagation cannot supply","D":"Scaling up one layer rate automatically scales up every other layer rate by the same amount","ans":"B","exp":"The remedy is to repair the per-layer factors with ReLU, He initialisation, normalisation or a skip connection, because a signal buried in numerical noise carries no usable direction however much you multiply it. The answer \"Scaling up one layer rate automatically scales up every other layer rate\" is the objection to a single global rate, not to per-layer rates. The answer \"Per-layer learning rates cannot be expressed in torch.optim at all\" is false, since parameter groups do exactly that. The answer \"Each layer would then need its own loss function\" is wrong because one loss already supplies a gradient to every layer."},

  // Week 5 (105 - 130)
  {"num":105,"week":5,"q":"A network is trained with dropout at p = 0.2. What must happen to the activations at inference time?","A":"All units are used once, unscaled - training already divided the kept activations by 0.8","B":"Ten stochastic forward passes are averaged and the mean prediction is reported","C":"Every activation is multiplied by 0.2, so the scale matches training","D":"Twenty per cent of the activations are dropped again, so training and inference see the same network","ans":"A","exp":"Inverted dropout already divided every kept activation by 1 - p = 0.8 during training, so the expected output matches the full deterministic output and model.eval() simply uses every unit once. \"Twenty per cent of the...\" is the careful-sounding instinct the slides warn about - leaving the model in train() mode. \"Ten stochastic forward passes are...\" is Monte Carlo dropout, a real technique but not ordinary inference."},
  {"num":106,"week":5,"q":"What is the defining symptom of overfitting?","A":"Training loss keeps falling while validation loss starts rising","B":"Validation loss falls faster than training loss","C":"The gradients become very small","D":"Both training and validation loss stay high","ans":"A","exp":"The model is still improving on data it has seen and getting worse on data it has not: it is memorising rather than generalising. \"Both training and validation loss...\" is underfitting. \"Validation loss falls faster than...\" normally means the validation set is easier or leakier than the training set."},
  {"num":107,"week":5,"q":"Why must the test set be touched only once, at the very end?","A":"Because every decision made after looking at it leaks information and the score stops being an honest estimate","B":"Because the loss cannot be computed on it more than once","C":"Because it has no labels","D":"Because it is usually smaller than the validation set","ans":"A","exp":"Tune on the test set and it quietly becomes a second validation set: the number you report is then an estimate of how well you tuned, not of how well the model generalises. The validation set exists to absorb exactly that tuning."},
  {"num":108,"week":5,"q":"What does L2 regularisation add to the loss, and what effect does it have?","A":"The sum of the squared weights, which pulls weights towards zero","B":"The sum of the absolute weights, which drives many weights exactly to zero","C":"The number of parameters, which penalises depth","D":"The validation loss, which keeps the two in step","ans":"A","exp":"L2 adds lambda times the sum of w squared, so large weights are expensive and the fit is kept smooth. \"The sum of the absolute...\" describes L1, whose constant-size gradient is what actually zeroes weights out; L2 shrinks them but rarely reaches zero."},
  {"num":109,"week":5,"q":"A model scores 0.99 accuracy on a dataset where 99 per cent of the samples are the negative class. What has it most likely learned?","A":"To predict the majority class every time","B":"To overfit the positive class","C":"Nothing can be said without the learning rate","D":"A genuinely excellent decision rule","ans":"A","exp":"Always answering \"negative\" scores exactly the base rate, which is why accuracy is the wrong headline number on an imbalanced problem. Precision, recall or F1 on the positive class would expose it immediately."},
  {"num":110,"week":5,"q":"What is the point of a validation set, as distinct from the training set?","A":"To choose between models and settings using data the weights were never fitted on","B":"To increase the total amount of data the weights see","C":"To replace the test set entirely","D":"To provide extra gradient steps once the training set is exhausted","ans":"A","exp":"The weights are fitted on the training set; every choice ABOUT the model - when to stop, which architecture, which learning rate - is made on the validation set. \"To provide extra gradient steps...\" and \"To increase the total amount...\" both describe it as more training data, which destroys its purpose."},
  {"num":111,"week":5,"q":"Early stopping halts training when which quantity stops improving?","A":"Validation loss","B":"The learning rate","C":"The gradient norm","D":"Training loss","ans":"A","exp":"Training loss almost always keeps falling, so stopping on it would never trigger. Validation loss is the one that turns upwards when memorising begins, and that turn is the signal."},
  {"num":112,"week":5,"q":"Data augmentation improves generalisation mainly because it","A":"shows the model new variations of each example, so it cannot rely on incidental details","B":"makes each epoch run faster","C":"removes the need for a validation set","D":"reduces the number of parameters in the network","ans":"A","exp":"Random crops and flips mean the same photograph is never seen twice in exactly the same form, so memorising an exact pixel arrangement stops paying. The parameter count is untouched, and epochs get slower, not faster."},
  {"num":113,"week":5,"q":"A test set of 1,000 cases gives the confusion matrix TP = 30, FP = 20, FN = 50, TN = 900. What is the accuracy?","A":"37.5%","B":"60.0%","C":"90.0%","D":"93.0%","ans":"D","exp":"Accuracy is the diagonal over the total, (TP + TN)/N = (30 + 900)/1000 = 93.0%. The answer \"37.5%\" is the recall, 30/(30 + 50). The answer \"60.0%\" is the precision, 30/(30 + 20). The answer \"90.0%\" counts only the true negatives and forgets to add the 30 true positives to the diagonal."},
  {"num":114,"week":5,"q":"A test set of 1,000 cases gives the confusion matrix TP = 24, FP = 16, FN = 36, TN = 924. What is the precision?","A":"0.316","B":"0.400","C":"0.600","D":"0.948","ans":"C","exp":"Precision is TP over everything flagged, 24/(24 + 16) = 0.600. The answer \"0.400\" is the recall, 24/(24 + 36), which divides by everything truly positive instead. The answer \"0.948\" is the accuracy, inflated by the 924 true negatives. The answer \"0.316\" puts both FP and FN in the denominator, 24/76, which is neither metric."},
  {"num":115,"week":5,"q":"A test set of 1,000 cases gives the confusion matrix TP = 21, FP = 9, FN = 29, TN = 941. What is the recall?","A":"0.356","B":"0.420","C":"0.700","D":"0.962","ans":"B","exp":"Recall is TP over everything truly positive, 21/(21 + 29) = 0.420. The answer \"0.700\" is the precision, 21/(21 + 9). The answer \"0.962\" is the accuracy, which the 941 true negatives dominate. The answer \"0.356\" is 21/59, which wrongly adds the false positives to the denominator as well."},
  {"num":116,"week":5,"q":"A model gives TP = 20, FP = 20 and FN = 60 on a test set, so its precision is 0.500 and its recall is 0.250. What is its F1 score?","A":"0.250","B":"0.333","C":"0.375","D":"0.500","ans":"B","exp":"F1 = 2PR/(P + R) = 2 x 0.500 x 0.250 / 0.750 = 0.333. The answer \"0.375\" is the arithmetic mean of the two, which averages the pain away instead of collapsing toward the weaker number. The answer \"0.250\" simply repeats the recall and the answer \"0.500\" repeats the precision, so neither combines them at all."},
  {"num":117,"week":5,"q":"Inverted dropout is used with p = 0.25. By what factor is each kept activation divided during a training forward pass, expressed as a multiplier?","A":"0.250","B":"0.750","C":"1.333","D":"4.000","ans":"C","exp":"Kept activations are divided by 1 - p = 0.75, which is the same as multiplying by 1/(1 - p) = 1.333. The answer \"0.750\" is 1 - p itself, the divisor rather than the multiplier. The answer \"0.250\" is p, the drop probability. The answer \"4.000\" is 1/p, which uses the drop probability where the keep probability belongs."},
  {"num":118,"week":5,"q":"A dense layer of 400 units is trained with dropout at p = 0.3. How many units are kept on average in one forward pass?","A":"120","B":"280","C":"300","D":"400","ans":"B","exp":"Each unit is kept with probability 1 - p = 0.7, so the expected count is 400 x 0.7 = 280. The answer \"120\" is the expected number dropped, 400 x 0.3. The answer \"300\" uses a keep probability of 0.75 rather than 0.7. The answer \"400\" is the layer width, which is the count only at inference, when dropout is off."},
  {"num":119,"week":5,"q":"A layer holds the three weights 0.5, -1.0 and 1.5. With lambda = 0.2, what does the L2 term (lambda/2) x sum of w_i^2 add to the loss?","A":"0.350","B":"0.600","C":"0.700","D":"3.500","ans":"A","exp":"The squared norm is 0.25 + 1.00 + 2.25 = 3.50, and (0.2/2) x 3.50 = 0.350. The answer \"0.700\" drops the factor of one half. The answer \"0.600\" uses the sum of absolute values, 0.2 x 3.0, which is the L1 penalty. The answer \"3.500\" is the squared norm on its own, with lambda never applied."},
  {"num":120,"week":5,"q":"A dataset of 12,000 labelled examples is split 70/20/10 into training, validation and test. How many examples does the validation split hold?","A":"1,200","B":"2,400","C":"8,400","D":"10,800","ans":"B","exp":"Twenty per cent of 12,000 is 2,400 examples. The answer \"1,200\" is the 10 per cent test split. The answer \"8,400\" is the 70 per cent training split. The answer \"10,800\" adds training and validation together, which is everything the weights and the hyperparameters are allowed to see, not the validation split itself."},
  {"num":121,"week":5,"q":"A test set contains 2.5 per cent positives. What accuracy does a predictor that answers negative for every case achieve on it?","A":"2.5%","B":"50.0%","C":"97.5%","D":"99.0%","ans":"C","exp":"The always-negative baseline scores 1 - prevalence = 1 - 0.025 = 97.5%, which is why that figure must be quoted next to any reported accuracy. The answer \"2.5%\" is the prevalence itself, the fraction it gets wrong. The answer \"50.0%\" is chance on a balanced problem. The answer \"99.0%\" is the baseline for a 1 per cent prevalence, not 2.5 per cent."},
  {"num":122,"week":5,"q":"Decoupled weight decay shrinks a weight by the factor 1 - eta*lambda each step. A weight starts at 0.80 with a data gradient of exactly zero, eta = 0.01 and lambda = 0.1. What is it after 1,000 steps?","A":"0.294","B":"0.720","C":"0.799","D":"0.800","ans":"A","exp":"The per-step factor is 1 - 0.01 x 0.1 = 0.999, and 0.80 x 0.999^1000 = 0.80 x 0.368 = 0.294, so an unused weight decays geometrically. The answer \"0.799\" applies the shrinkage once instead of 1,000 times. The answer \"0.720\" uses a factor of 1 - lambda = 0.9 and ignores eta. The answer \"0.800\" assumes a zero data gradient leaves the weight untouched."},
  {"num":123,"week":5,"q":"In the decomposition E[(y - f_hat(x))^2] = Bias[f_hat]^2 + Var[f_hat] + sigma^2, what does the sigma^2 term represent?","A":"The gap between training accuracy and validation accuracy at the final epoch","B":"Irreducible label noise, a floor that no architecture and no amount of data removes","C":"The error from a model family too simple to represent the true function","D":"The error from fitting the noise of one particular training sample","ans":"B","exp":"sigma^2 is the irreducible noise in the labels themselves, so it bounds how well any model can do. The option \"The error from a model family too simple\" is the bias term. The option \"The error from fitting the noise of one particular training sample\" is the variance term. The option \"The gap between training accuracy and validation\" is a measured symptom, not a term in the decomposition."},
  {"num":124,"week":5,"q":"As model capacity increases while the dataset size is held fixed, how do the bias and variance terms behave?","A":"Bias and variance are unchanged; only the irreducible noise term moves","B":"Bias falls and variance rises","C":"Bias rises and variance falls","D":"Bias and variance both fall together","ans":"B","exp":"A richer model family can represent more functions, so bias falls, but it has more freedom to fit the noise of this particular sample, so variance rises. The option \"Bias rises and variance falls\" reverses both. The option \"Bias and variance both fall together\" would mean capacity is free. The option \"Bias and variance are unchanged\" is wrong because sigma^2 is the one term capacity cannot touch."},
  {"num":125,"week":5,"q":"Which pattern in the training and validation loss curves is the signature of underfitting?","A":"Validation loss sits below training loss for the whole run","B":"Training loss plateaus high and validation loss tracks it closely, both poor and nearly equal","C":"Validation loss swings up and down sharply from one epoch to the next","D":"Training loss keeps falling while validation loss bottoms out and climbs","ans":"B","exp":"Underfitting is high bias: the model cannot even fit its training set, so both curves settle high and close together. The option \"Training loss keeps falling while validation loss bottoms out\" is the overfitting signature instead. The option \"Validation loss sits below training loss\" is the normal effect of dropout being active only in training. The option \"Validation loss swings up and down\" points at an optimisation setting, not at capacity."},
  {"num":126,"week":5,"q":"L1 regularisation adds the sum of the absolute weights to the loss. What does it do to the learnt weights that L2 does not?","A":"It penalises the biases and the normalisation parameters rather than the weights","B":"It drives some weights exactly to zero, so the solution becomes sparse","C":"It spreads weight across correlated features instead of removing any of them","D":"It shrinks every weight by the same multiplicative fraction on every step","ans":"B","exp":"L1 has a constant-size pull toward zero, so weights that earn little reach exactly zero and the solution is sparse. The option \"It spreads weight across correlated features\" is what L2 does. The option \"It shrinks every weight by the same multiplicative fraction\" describes L2 decay. The option \"It penalises the biases and the normalisation parameters\" is wrong because those are the parameters you exclude from decay."},
  {"num":127,"week":5,"q":"Why is early stopping described as a form of capacity control rather than only a time saver?","A":"It reduces the number of parameters stored in the saved checkpoint","B":"Fewer optimisation steps means a smaller set of functions the optimiser can actually reach","C":"It lowers the learning rate until the weights can no longer change at all","D":"It deletes units from the network once validation loss stops improving","ans":"B","exp":"Effective capacity depends on how many steps the optimiser takes, so cutting the steps shrinks the set of reachable functions. The option \"It deletes units from the network\" is wrong because the architecture is untouched. The option \"It reduces the number of parameters stored\" is wrong for the same reason: the checkpoint has the full parameter set. The option \"It lowers the learning rate\" confuses early stopping with a schedule."},
  {"num":128,"week":5,"q":"Which splits may augmentation transforms be applied to, and why?","A":"The test split only, so that the final estimate covers more variation","B":"The training split only, because augmenting validation or test makes the measured score meaningless","C":"Every split, so that training and evaluation see the same distribution of images","D":"The validation split only, because it is used for tuning rather than for the final score","ans":"B","exp":"Augmentation manufactures training examples; a held-out split has to stay a fixed, honest sample, so only the training split is transformed. The option \"Every split\" destroys the comparability of the metric between runs. The option \"The validation split only\" and the option \"The test split only\" both corrupt exactly the data whose job is to measure, while leaving the training set unchanged."},
  {"num":129,"week":5,"q":"Below a few thousand examples, what does 5-fold cross-validation give that a single validation split does not?","A":"A guarantee that the model cannot overfit any individual fold","B":"Five estimates in which every example is validated exactly once, so the comparison is far less noisy","C":"Five times as much training data, because the folds are concatenated","D":"A final test score, so that no separate held-out test set is needed","ans":"B","exp":"Each example serves in validation exactly once across the five folds, so the averaged estimate is much less noisy than one small split. The option \"Five times as much training data\" is wrong because each fold still trains on four fifths of the data. The option \"A final test score\" is wrong because tuning still happens on these folds. The option \"A guarantee that the model cannot overfit\" is not something any protocol provides."},
  {"num":130,"week":5,"q":"A screening test set holds 1,000 parts, 4 of them faulty, and the confusion matrix is TP = 2, FP = 3, FN = 2, TN = 993. The accuracy is 99.5 per cent. Why is that figure uninformative?","A":"Accuracy cannot be computed when the classes are imbalanced, so the 99.5 per cent figure is undefined","B":"Almost all of it comes from the 993 true negatives, and flagging nothing at all would score 99.6 per cent","C":"The 3 false positives are weighted more heavily than the 2 false negatives in the accuracy formula","D":"Accuracy is a threshold-free metric, so it scores the ranking rather than the predictions actually made","ans":"B","exp":"The do-nothing model that passes every part scores 996/1000 = 99.6 per cent, so 99.5 per cent is worse than doing nothing, and the precision of 0.400 and recall of 0.500 are what the accuracy hid. The option \"Accuracy cannot be computed\" is false; it is defined, just dominated. The option \"The 3 false positives are weighted more heavily\" is wrong because accuracy weights every case equally. The option \"Accuracy is a threshold-free metric\" describes average precision, not accuracy."},

  // Week 6 (131 - 152)
  {"num":131,"week":6,"q":"A long training run is interrupted and has to resume exactly where it stopped. Besides the model weights, what must the saved checkpoint contain?","A":"The optimiser state and the learning-rate scheduler position, so that neither the running statistics nor the schedule starts over","B":"The gradients computed on the last batch, which the next update needs in order to continue","C":"A copy of the training set, so that the resumed run sees the examples in the same order","D":"Only the model weights - everything else is rebuilt from the configuration file when training restarts","ans":"A","exp":"A checkpoint carries weights, optimiser state, scheduler position and, in a mixed-precision run, the loss scaler. \"Only the model weights -...\" is the thin checkpoint: reload weights alone and Adam's moment estimates rebuild from nothing while the scheduler jumps back to its opening rate, which shows as a visible jump in the loss. \"The gradients computed on the...\" saves something the next backward pass recomputes anyway."},
  {"num":132,"week":6,"q":"In the Week 6 pipeline, what does a DataLoader do that a Dataset does not?","A":"It batches examples, shuffles their order and prefetches them with worker processes","B":"It computes the loss for each batch","C":"It decides how many epochs the run lasts","D":"It reads a single example from disk and returns it","ans":"A","exp":"A map-style Dataset answers one question only - \"give me example i\" - and hands back a single (image, label) pair. Batching, shuffle order and worker processes all belong to the DataLoader wrapped around it. \"It computes the loss for...\" and \"It decides how many epochs...\" belong to the training loop."},
  {"num":133,"week":6,"q":"Which of these must be true inside a validation loop?","A":"model.eval() is set and gradients are switched off","B":"The data is shuffled, exactly as in training","C":"Augmentation is applied, so validation is as hard as training","D":"The model stays in train() mode so the behaviour matches training","ans":"A","exp":"eval() switches dropout off and makes batch-norm use its running statistics; no_grad() stops the graph being built. Shuffling a validation set changes nothing about the score, and augmenting it makes the number unrepeatable."},
  {"num":134,"week":6,"q":"Why is setting a random seed useful when developing a model?","A":"It makes a run repeatable, so a change in the score can be attributed to the change you made","B":"It removes the need for a validation set","C":"It speeds up data loading","D":"It makes the model more accurate","ans":"A","exp":"Without a fixed seed the initialisation and the shuffle differ every run, so you cannot tell whether a score moved because of your change or because of luck. It does not make anything more accurate, only comparable."},
  {"num":135,"week":6,"q":"Gradient accumulation lets you","A":"train with a larger effective batch size than fits in memory","B":"skip the backward pass on some batches","C":"use a larger learning rate safely","D":"avoid saving checkpoints","ans":"A","exp":"Backward is run on several small batches and the gradients are summed before one optimiser step, which gives the gradient of the larger batch without ever holding it in memory. The backward pass is run every time, so \"skip the backward pass on...\" is wrong."},
  {"num":136,"week":6,"q":"A run shows training loss falling smoothly while validation loss is extremely noisy from epoch to epoch. What is the most likely plain explanation?","A":"The validation set is small, so each epoch samples a different handful of hard examples","B":"The optimiser has diverged","C":"Dropout has been left on during validation","D":"The learning rate is too small","ans":"A","exp":"With only a few hundred validation samples, one or two awkward examples move the average a lot. Note that \"Dropout has been left on...\" would also make validation noisy, which is why it is a tempting answer, but \"Dropout has been left on...\" produces noise AND a worse mean, while a small set produces noise around a steady mean."},
  {"num":137,"week":6,"q":"A training set of 9,000 images is loaded with batch_size=128 and drop_last=True. How many batches does one epoch yield?","A":"70","B":"71","C":"72","D":"128","ans":"A","exp":"floor(9000/128) = 70 full batches of 128, and drop_last discards the leftover 40 images. The answer \"71\" is the count you get with drop_last=False, which keeps a short final batch. \"72\" over-counts by rounding twice, and \"128\" is the batch size rather than the number of batches."},
  {"num":138,"week":6,"q":"Which pair of methods is the entire contract a map-style Dataset must implement to work with a DataLoader?","A":"__iter__ and __next__","B":"__len__ and __getitem__","C":"train and eval","D":"__init__ and forward","ans":"B","exp":"A map-style Dataset needs __len__ to report its size and __getitem__ to return the example at one index; anything implementing those two works with a DataLoader. The answer \"__iter__ and __next__\" is the iterable-style contract instead. \"__init__ and forward\" belongs to a model, and \"train and eval\" are mode switches on nn.Module, not dataset methods."},
  {"num":139,"week":6,"q":"A Dataset returns one example as a 3x224x224 float tensor plus an integer label. With batch_size=16, what shape does the image tensor have after the DataLoader collates one batch?","A":"3x224x224","B":"16x3x224x224","C":"16x224x224x3","D":"3x16x224x224","ans":"B","exp":"Collate stacks the 16 single examples along a new leading batch dimension, giving 16x3x224x224. The answer \"3x224x224\" is the shape of one example before batching. \"3x16x224x224\" stacks along the wrong axis, and \"16x224x224x3\" is the channels-last layout used by TensorFlow rather than the channels-first tensor this Dataset returns."},
  {"num":140,"week":6,"q":"What distinguishes an iterable-style dataset from a map-style one?","A":"It can only be used when the labels are one-hot encoded","B":"It yields samples from __iter__ and offers no length and no random access by index","C":"It applies augmentation automatically, so no transform has to be written","D":"It stores the whole dataset in GPU memory before training begins","ans":"B","exp":"An iterable-style dataset defines __iter__ and yields samples, so there is no length and no fetching by index; that is why it suits streams and remote shards, and why shuffling becomes a bounded buffer rather than a permutation. \"It stores the whole dataset in GPU memory before training begins\" describes caching, not the interface. \"It applies augmentation automatically, so no transform has to be written\" and \"It can only be used when the labels are one-hot encoded\" are not part of either contract."},
  {"num":141,"week":6,"q":"A run uses a micro-batch of 24 images and calls the optimiser step once every 8 backward passes. What effective batch size does each optimiser step see?","A":"3","B":"24","C":"32","D":"192","ans":"D","exp":"Gradient accumulation sums the gradients of 8 micro-batches of 24, so one step sees 24 x 8 = 192 examples. The answer \"24\" is the micro-batch that actually occupies memory, \"3\" divides instead of multiplying, and \"32\" adds the two numbers."},
  {"num":142,"week":6,"q":"Why does a PyTorch training loop call opt.zero_grad() before loss.backward() on each batch?","A":"Because the optimiser cannot read the learning rate until the gradients are cleared","B":"Because backward() adds gradients into .grad, so without clearing them the new batch is updated with the previous batch gradients included","C":"Because it releases the GPU memory held by the model weights","D":"Because it resets the model parameters to their initial values","ans":"B","exp":"backward() accumulates into the .grad buffers rather than overwriting them, so each batch must start from zero unless accumulation is intended. The answer \"Because it resets the model parameters to their initial values\" confuses gradients with weights, \"Because it releases the GPU memory held by the model weights\" confuses them with the parameter store, and \"Because the optimiser cannot read the learning rate until the gradients are cleared\" invents a dependency that does not exist."},
  {"num":143,"week":6,"q":"A validation set of 4,500 images is loaded with batch_size=32 and drop_last=False. How many batches does one pass over it produce?","A":"140","B":"141","C":"142","D":"4500","ans":"B","exp":"4500/32 = 140.625, so there are 140 full batches plus a final short batch of 20 images, giving 141. The answer \"140\" is the count under drop_last=True, which would silently discard 20 validation images. \"142\" rounds up twice, and \"4500\" is the number of examples."},
  {"num":144,"week":6,"q":"What does raising num_workers in a DataLoader change?","A":"It splits the model across several GPUs","B":"It decodes and transforms samples in parallel worker processes, so CPU work overlaps GPU compute","C":"It lowers the learning rate in proportion to the number of processes","D":"It increases the batch size automatically to fill the GPU","ans":"B","exp":"num_workers sets how many separate processes run __getitem__, so reading, decoding and augmenting happen in parallel and overlap with the GPU step; each worker also costs host RAM, so more is not always faster. The answer \"It splits the model across several GPUs\" describes model parallelism, while \"It increases the batch size automatically to fill the GPU\" and \"It lowers the learning rate in proportion to the number of processes\" are not things the loader touches at all."},
  {"num":145,"week":6,"q":"A 12,000-example training set is loaded with batch_size=50 and drop_last=True, and the run lasts 15 epochs. How many optimiser steps does the whole run perform?","A":"240","B":"800","C":"3600","D":"180000","ans":"C","exp":"One epoch is 12000/50 = 240 steps, and 240 x 15 = 3600 over the run. The answer \"240\" counts a single epoch, \"800\" divides the examples by the epoch count instead of the batch size, and \"180000\" multiplies examples by epochs without ever dividing by the batch size."},
  {"num":146,"week":6,"q":"Why must a validation loader not be built with drop_last=True?","A":"It would stop the loss from being differentiable","B":"It would prevent the model from leaving train() mode","C":"It would throw away the final short batch, so the metric is measured on fewer examples and shifts when the batch size changes","D":"It would make BatchNorm use batch statistics instead of its running statistics","ans":"C","exp":"Validation has to be the same fixed set every epoch, and dropping the last partial batch removes examples from the measurement in a way that depends on the batch size. The answer \"It would make BatchNorm use batch statistics instead of its running statistics\" is the job of model.eval(), not drop_last. \"It would prevent the model from leaving train() mode\" and \"It would stop the loss from being differentiable\" describe effects drop_last simply does not have."},
  {"num":147,"week":6,"q":"One batch of 64 RGB images at 256x256 resolution is held as a float32 tensor. Taking 1 MB as 1,048,576 bytes, how much memory does that batch occupy?","A":"16 MB","B":"24 MB","C":"48 MB","D":"144 MB","ans":"C","exp":"64 x 3 x 256 x 256 x 4 bytes = 50,331,648 bytes, which is 48 MB. The answer \"16 MB\" forgets the three colour channels, \"24 MB\" is what the same batch would cost stored in half precision, and \"144 MB\" multiplies by the three channels a second time."},
  {"num":148,"week":6,"q":"A pipeline writes both best.pt and last.pt every epoch. What is the difference between the two files?","A":"best.pt stores the configuration and the metrics only, while last.pt stores the tensors","B":"best.pt is saved in half precision to save disk space, while last.pt is saved in full precision","C":"best.pt holds the state from the epoch with the best validation score and is what you report from, while last.pt holds the most recent state and is what a crashed run resumes from","D":"best.pt holds the weights and last.pt holds the optimiser state, so both are needed to resume","ans":"C","exp":"The two files answer two different questions: the best validation epoch is the one you report, and the newest epoch is the one a resume must continue from, and those are usually not the same epoch. The answer \"best.pt holds the weights and last.pt holds the optimiser state, so both are needed to resume\" is wrong because each file is a complete checkpoint on its own. \"best.pt is saved in half precision to save disk space, while last.pt is saved in full precision\" and \"best.pt stores the configuration and the metrics only, while last.pt stores the tensors\" invent a split that the saving code does not make."},
  {"num":149,"week":6,"q":"A training set of 7,000 images is loaded with batch_size=128 and drop_last=True. How many images go unused at the end of each epoch?","A":"54","B":"88","C":"128","D":"6912","ans":"B","exp":"7000/128 gives 54 full batches covering 54 x 128 = 6912 images, so 7000 - 6912 = 88 images are dropped. The answer \"54\" is the number of batches, \"6912\" is the number of images actually seen, and \"128\" is the batch size rather than the remainder."},
  {"num":150,"week":6,"q":"Why fix a train/validation split with a saved index file rather than by relying on a random seed alone?","A":"Because an index file makes the split stratified by class automatically","B":"Because a seed is designed to give a different split on every machine","C":"Because the index file reproduces the same split even if a library upgrade changes how the random number generator draws","D":"Because a seed can only be set once per script, so a second split would be unseeded","ans":"C","exp":"A seed only reproduces a split through a particular generator, and an upgrade that changes the generator quietly changes the split, so the saved list of indices is the durable record. The answer \"Because an index file makes the split stratified by class automatically\" confuses storing a split with constructing one. \"Because a seed can only be set once per script, so a second split would be unseeded\" and \"Because a seed is designed to give a different split on every machine\" misdescribe what seeding does."},
  {"num":151,"week":6,"q":"A 100M-parameter network trains with Adam in fp32. Taking 1 GB as 1,000,000,000 bytes, how much memory do the weights, the gradients and the two Adam moment buffers need in total?","A":"0.4 GB","B":"0.8 GB","C":"1.6 GB","D":"3.2 GB","ans":"C","exp":"Each fp32 parameter costs 4 bytes for the weight, 4 for its gradient and 8 for the two moments, so 16 bytes in total: 100e6 x 16 = 1.6 GB, and this term does not depend on batch size. The answer \"0.4 GB\" counts the weights alone, \"0.8 GB\" counts weights and gradients but omits the moment buffers, and \"3.2 GB\" doubles the correct figure as if mixed precision added a second copy."},
  {"num":152,"week":6,"q":"In an iterable-style dataset used with num_workers=4, why must __iter__ consult get_worker_info()?","A":"Without it the samples arrive on the CPU instead of on the GPU","B":"Without it the shuffle buffer grows until the host runs out of memory","C":"Without it every worker iterates the whole stream, so each sample is emitted four times","D":"Without it the DataLoader cannot compute len() and raises an error","ans":"C","exp":"Each worker runs its own copy of __iter__, so unless the stream is sharded by worker id every worker yields the same data and the epoch contains four copies of every sample. The answer \"Without it the DataLoader cannot compute len() and raises an error\" is wrong because an iterable-style dataset has no length either way. \"Without it the samples arrive on the CPU instead of on the GPU\" describes the device transfer, and \"Without it the shuffle buffer grows until the host runs out of memory\" describes a buffer size that get_worker_info does not control."},

  // Week 7 (153 - 178)
  {"num":153,"week":7,"q":"A 2x2 max-pooling layer with stride 2 is applied to a 32x32x16 feature map. What is the output shape?","A":"16x16x8","B":"32x32x16","C":"16x16x64","D":"16x16x16","ans":"D","exp":"A 2x2 window with stride 2 halves each spatial dimension, 32 -> 16, and pooling acts channel by channel, so the 16 channels pass through untouched. \"16x16x8\" halves the channels as well, confusing pooling with a 1x1 convolution. \"16x16x64\" applies the \"double the channels\" design convention, which is the job of the next convolution."},
  {"num":154,"week":7,"q":"Why does a 3x3 convolutional layer need far fewer parameters than a dense layer producing the same number of features?","A":"Convolutional layers carry no bias terms, and the biases are most of a dense layer count","B":"The pooling layer after it removes most of the convolution parameters","C":"A convolution stores its weights at lower numerical precision than a dense layer does","D":"One kernel is reused at every spatial position, so the same weights serve the whole feature map","ans":"D","exp":"Parameter sharing: one kernel is trained by all 1,024 positions of every image, which is why 896 parameters produce the same 32x32x32 volume that a dense layer needs over 100 million weights for. \"Convolutional layers carry no bias...\" is false - convolutions do have one bias per output channel. \"The pooling layer after it...\" confuses pooling, which has zero parameters, with the saving."},
  {"num":155,"week":7,"q":"A 3x3 convolution with padding 1 and stride 1 is applied to a 64x64x3 input and produces 16 filters. How many learnable parameters does it have, biases included?","A":"432","B":"448","C":"1,296","D":"12,288","ans":"B","exp":"Params = K x K x C_in x C_out + C_out = 3 x 3 x 3 x 16 + 16 = 432 + 16 = 448. \"432\" drops the bias term. \"12,288\" brings the spatial size into the count, which never appears in the formula - only kernel size and the two channel counts do."},
  {"num":156,"week":7,"q":"A 5x5 convolution with padding 2 and stride 1 is applied to a 48x48x1 grayscale input with 16 filters. What is the output height, and how many parameters?","A":"44 and 416","B":"48 and 400","C":"48 and 416","D":"48 and 1,216","ans":"C","exp":"P = 2 with K = 5 is the \"same\" setting, P = (K-1)/2, so the height is unchanged at 48. Params = 5 x 5 x 1 x 16 + 16 = 400 + 16 = 416. \"48 and 400\" drops the bias; \"48 and 1,216\" uses C_in = 3 as if the image were still in colour."},
  {"num":157,"week":7,"q":"What does padding of P = 1 with a 3x3 kernel and stride 1 achieve?","A":"It doubles the number of channels","B":"It removes the bias terms","C":"It halves the spatial size","D":"It leaves the spatial size unchanged","ans":"D","exp":"This is the \"same\" setting, P = (K-1)/2, so downsampling only happens where you choose it rather than by accident. Without padding a 3x3 kernel loses one pixel from each side every layer."},
  {"num":158,"week":7,"q":"A classification CNN is fed the same photograph shifted three pixels to the right. What happens inside the convolutional layers?","A":"The feature maps are completely different, because the weights are tied to absolute positions","B":"Nothing changes at all, because convolution is fully invariant to position","C":"The parameter count changes","D":"Every feature map shifts by three pixels as well","ans":"D","exp":"Convolution is equivariant, not invariant: shift the input and the response shifts with it, which preserves where the feature was found. \"The feature maps are completely...\" describes a dense layer, whose weights really are tied to absolute pixels. Full invariance only arrives later, via pooling and global pooling."},
  {"num":159,"week":7,"q":"A student proposes raising the kernel from 3x3 to 7x7 to give the network more context. What does that cost?","A":"Parameters double","B":"The output gains channels","C":"Nothing; parameters depend only on the channel counts","D":"Parameters and compute grow with K squared, so roughly 5.4 times as much","ans":"D","exp":"49/9 is about 5.4, and both the weight count and the multiply-accumulate count scale with it. Stacking small kernels and downsampling is the cheaper way to enlarge the receptive field, which is why modern networks use 3x3 almost everywhere."},
  {"num":160,"week":7,"q":"A volume of 8x8x64 is flattened before a dense layer. What is the length of the resulting vector?","A":"64","B":"80","C":"512","D":"4,096","ans":"D","exp":"8 x 8 x 64 = 4,096: the product of all three dimensions. The answer \"80\" adds them, 8 + 8 + 64. \"512\" uses 8 x 64 and forgets a dimension. \"64\" keeps only the channel count, which would be the result of a global average pool instead of a flatten."},
  {"num":161,"week":7,"q":"A 5x5 convolution with no padding and stride 1 runs on a 28x28x1 input and holds 32 filters. What is the output volume?","A":"32x32x32","B":"24x24x1","C":"24x24x32","D":"28x28x32","ans":"C","exp":"H_out = floor((28 + 0 - 1*(5-1) - 1)/1) + 1 = 24, and the output depth is the number of filters, so the volume is 24x24x32. The answer \"28x28x32\" assumes the size is preserved, which only happens with P = 2 for a 5x5 kernel. \"24x24x1\" keeps the single input channel instead of the filter count. \"32x32x32\" reads the filter count as a spatial size as well."},
  {"num":162,"week":7,"q":"A 3x3 convolution with dilation 2, no padding and stride 1 runs on a 20x20 feature map. What is the output height?","A":"14","B":"16","C":"18","D":"20","ans":"B","exp":"H_out = floor((20 + 0 - 2*(3-1) - 1)/1) + 1 = 15 + 1 = 16, because dilation 2 gives the 3x3 kernel a 5x5 reach. The answer \"18\" is what an undilated 3x3 valid convolution would give, ignoring D altogether. \"14\" uses D*K = 6 instead of D*(K-1) = 4 for the kernel term. \"20\" assumes dilation leaves the spatial size alone, which is only true when the padding is raised to match."},
  {"num":163,"week":7,"q":"A 7x7 convolution with stride 2 and padding 3 is used as the stem on a 224x224x3 photograph. What is the output height?","A":"109","B":"111","C":"112","D":"224","ans":"C","exp":"H_out = floor((224 + 2*3 - 1*(7-1) - 1)/2) + 1 = floor(223/2) + 1 = 111 + 1 = 112. The answer \"111\" is floor(223/2) with the final +1 dropped. \"109\" is the same stem run with P = 0 instead of P = 3. \"224\" assumes that padding preserves the size whatever the stride, but stride 2 halves it."},
  {"num":164,"week":7,"q":"A 3x3 convolution maps 128 input channels to 256 output channels, with one bias per filter. How many learnable parameters does it hold?","A":"33,024","B":"147,584","C":"294,912","D":"295,168","ans":"D","exp":"Params = K*K*C_in*C_out + C_out = 3*3*128*256 + 256 = 294,912 + 256 = 295,168. The answer \"294,912\" drops the one bias per filter. \"33,024\" is the 1x1 version of the same channel change, with no spatial reach. \"147,584\" is a 3x3 convolution from 128 to 128 channels, so it halves the output depth."},
  {"num":165,"week":7,"q":"A 1x1 convolution cuts a 256-channel feature map down to 64 channels, with one bias per filter. How many parameters does it hold?","A":"320","B":"16,448","C":"65,536","D":"147,520","ans":"B","exp":"A 1x1 convolution is pure channel mixing: C_in*C_out + C_out = 256*64 + 64 = 16,448. The answer \"65,536\" squares the input channel count instead of multiplying the two different channel counts. \"320\" adds the two channel counts rather than multiplying them. \"147,520\" is the 3x3 version of the same channel change, which buys spatial reach the 1x1 does not have."},
  {"num":166,"week":7,"q":"A 5x5 convolution maps 16 channels to 32 channels on a 24x24 input, one bias per filter. How many parameters does it hold?","A":"832","B":"12,800","C":"12,832","D":"7,372,800","ans":"C","exp":"Params = 5*5*16*32 + 32 = 12,800 + 32 = 12,832, and the 24x24 input size never enters the count. The answer \"12,800\" forgets the one bias per filter. \"832\" is 5*5*32 + 32, which leaves C_in out of the product. \"7,372,800\" multiplies by the 24x24 positions as well, which gives the layer multiply-accumulates rather than its weights."},
  {"num":167,"week":7,"q":"A 3x3 convolution maps 16 channels to 32 channels. Both channel counts are then doubled to 32 and 64 with the kernel unchanged. What is the new parameter count?","A":"4,640","B":"9,280","C":"18,496","D":"18,560","ans":"C","exp":"Params = 3*3*32*64 + 64 = 18,432 + 64 = 18,496: doubling both counts quadruples the weight product. The answer \"9,280\" doubles the old 4,640, which is what students expect when they read the count as linear in channels. \"4,640\" is the original layer, unchanged. \"18,560\" is exactly four times the old total, which over-counts the biases, since they grow only with C_out."},
  {"num":168,"week":7,"q":"A 6x6x256 feature volume is flattened before a dense head. How long is the resulting vector?","A":"36","B":"256","C":"268","D":"9,216","ans":"D","exp":"Flattening multiplies every axis: 6 * 6 * 256 = 9,216. The answer \"36\" is the spatial area only, with the channel axis forgotten, which is the classic shape mismatch at the flatten layer. \"256\" keeps only the channel count. \"268\" adds the three axes instead of multiplying them."},
  {"num":169,"week":7,"q":"A 3x3 convolution maps 16 channels to 32 channels and its output is 32x32. Using MACs = H_out x W_out x K x K x C_in x C_out, how many multiply-accumulates does one image cost?","A":"4,718,592","B":"9,437,184","C":"14,155,776","D":"37,748,736","ans":"A","exp":"MACs = 32*32 * 3*3 * 16 * 32 = 1,024 * 9 * 512 = 4,718,592. The answer \"9,437,184\" doubles the output channels to 64. \"14,155,776\" uses K*K*K = 27 in place of K*K = 9. \"37,748,736\" is the cost of a batch of eight images rather than the one image asked for."},
  {"num":170,"week":7,"q":"A dense layer takes a flattened 32x32x3 image and produces the same 16,384 features that a 3x3 convolution of 16 filters would. How many weights and biases does the dense layer hold?","A":"448","B":"3,072","C":"50,331,648","D":"50,348,032","ans":"D","exp":"A dense layer wires every input to every output: 3,072 * 16,384 = 50,331,648 weights plus 16,384 biases = 50,348,032. The answer \"50,331,648\" leaves the biases out. \"448\" is the convolution that produces the same feature volume, which is the point of the comparison. \"3,072\" is only the flattened input length, before any layer runs."},
  {"num":171,"week":7,"q":"A 3x3 stride-1 convolution, then a 2x2 stride-2 max-pool, then two more 3x3 stride-1 convolutions. Using r += (K-1)*j then j *= S from r = 1 and j = 1, how wide is the receptive field?","A":"8","B":"11","C":"12","D":"16","ans":"C","exp":"The first conv takes r to 3 at j = 1, the pool adds (2-1)*1 = 1 to give r = 4 and sets j = 2, and each later conv adds (3-1)*2 = 4, giving r = 12. The answer \"8\" adds (K-1) at every layer without ever using the jump. \"11\" skips the pool own (K-1)*j contribution while still doubling the jump. \"16\" doubles the jump after every layer rather than only after the stride-2 one."},
  {"num":172,"week":7,"q":"Three stacked 3x3 convolutions reach the same 7x7 region as one 7x7 convolution. How many kernel weights per channel pair does each option use?","A":"9 against 49","B":"18 against 25","C":"27 against 49","D":"49 against 27","ans":"C","exp":"Three 3x3 kernels hold 3*9 = 27 weights per channel pair against 7*7 = 49 for the single wide kernel, and the stack adds two extra ReLUs for free. The answer \"9 against 49\" counts only one of the three stacked layers. \"18 against 25\" is the two-layer case that reaches 5x5, not 7x7. \"49 against 27\" has the two figures the wrong way round."},
  {"num":173,"week":7,"q":"Which three structural assumptions does a convolutional layer build into the model?","A":"Channel independence, dropout between channels, and weight decay","B":"Batch normalisation, residual connections and global average pooling","C":"Local receptive fields, parameter sharing and translation equivariance","D":"Global receptive fields, one independent weight per pixel position, and rotation invariance","ans":"C","exp":"Convolution assumes that useful structure is local, that the same detector is worth reusing at every position, and that shifting the input should shift the output. The answer \"Global receptive fields, one independent weight per pixel position\" describes the dense layer convolution replaces. \"Channel independence, dropout between channels\" names regularisers, which are choices made on top of any layer. \"Batch normalisation, residual connections\" names later architectural additions, not assumptions of the operation itself."},
  {"num":174,"week":7,"q":"Convolution is equivariant to shifts rather than invariant to them. Which part of a CNN builds up invariance to small shifts?","A":"Weight decay on the convolution kernels, which shrinks position-specific weights","B":"The one bias per output channel, which absorbs the shift","C":"Pooling, striding and global average pooling, which make the representation steadily less sensitive to position","D":"The kernel flip that frameworks apply before summing over the window","ans":"C","exp":"Downsampling is what converts a shifting feature map into a representation that barely moves, and global average pooling discards position entirely. The answer \"The one bias per output channel\" only adds a constant and cannot undo a shift. \"The kernel flip that frameworks apply\" is wrong twice over: frameworks compute cross-correlation and never flip, and a flip would not change position sensitivity anyway. \"Weight decay on the convolution kernels\" shrinks weights without touching where features are reported."},
  {"num":175,"week":7,"q":"What does same padding, P = (K-1)/2, buy you, and what does it cost?","A":"It makes the layer invariant to translation, so translation augmentation is no longer needed","B":"It removes the need for pooling, because a padded feature map can no longer shrink","C":"It holds the feature-map size fixed and lets border pixels enter more windows, at the cost of a black border the first layer learns filters for","D":"It raises the parameter count in proportion to the padding width, which is the price of holding the size","ans":"C","exp":"Same padding fixes the shape so you decide when downsampling happens, and it stops corner pixels taking part in one window while central pixels take part in nine; the price is an invented black border that the first layer really does learn filters for. The answer \"It raises the parameter count in proportion to the padding width\" is false: parameters depend only on K and the two channel counts. \"It makes the layer invariant to translation\" confuses padding with invariance, which comes from downsampling. \"It removes the need for pooling\" is wrong because you can still downsample deliberately with a pool or a stride."},
  {"num":176,"week":7,"q":"A 3x3 filter is applied to a 64-channel feature map. What does that one filter hold, and what does it produce?","A":"Sixty-four independent 3x3 squares, each producing its own output feature map","B":"A single 3x3 square of weights, applied to every channel and producing 64 output maps","C":"A 3x3x64 block of weights plus one bias, producing a single output feature map","D":"A 3x3 square applied to the first channel only, with the other 63 channels ignored","ans":"C","exp":"The sum runs over channels as well as space, so one filter is a 3x3x64 block of 576 weights plus one bias, and the channel axis is summed away to leave one map. The answer \"Sixty-four independent 3x3 squares, each producing its own output feature map\" describes a depthwise convolution, which breaks this rule on purpose. \"A single 3x3 square of weights, applied to every channel\" would share nine weights across channels and is why students forget C_in from the parameter count. \"A 3x3 square applied to the first channel only\" would mean a convolution never mixes colour planes, which it always does."},
  {"num":177,"week":7,"q":"What does a 2x2 max-pool with stride 2 do, and how many parameters does it add?","A":"It halves the channel count and adds one bias per channel","B":"It keeps the strongest response in each window and adds four weights per channel","C":"It keeps the strongest response in each 2x2 window and adds no parameters","D":"It averages each 2x2 window and adds one weight per window","ans":"C","exp":"Max-pooling reports the largest response in each 2x2 window, halving height and width while holding zero parameters, since nothing about it is learned. The answer \"It keeps the strongest response in each window and adds four weights per channel\" gets the operation right but invents weights for it. \"It averages each 2x2 window\" describes average pooling, and that has no weights either. \"It halves the channel count\" is wrong: pooling acts spatially and leaves depth untouched."},
  {"num":178,"week":7,"q":"A 2x2 max-pool and a stride-2 convolution both halve resolution. What distinguishes the two?","A":"They are the same operation under two names, so the choice is only a matter of style","B":"The pool learns which responses to keep, while the strided convolution discards a fixed three quarters of them","C":"The pool holds no parameters and learns nothing, while the strided convolution learns what to keep at a cost of K*K*C_in*C_out + C_out weights","D":"The pool also halves the channel count, while the strided convolution leaves the depth unchanged","ans":"C","exp":"The pool is a fixed rule with zero parameters, while a stride-2 convolution pays for weights and in return learns how to downsample, which is why ResNet-style networks prefer it. The answer \"The pool learns which responses to keep\" has the two exactly the wrong way round. \"The pool also halves the channel count\" is false, since pooling never touches depth. \"They are the same operation under two names\" ignores both the learned weights and the fourfold drop in compute a stride brings."},

  // Week 8 (179 - 200)
  {"num":179,"week":8,"q":"In the feature-extraction setting of transfer learning, which parameters are updated during training?","A":"All of them, with the backbone held at a much smaller learning rate than the head","B":"The convolutional backbone only; the head keeps its pre-trained values","C":"None - feature extraction is a forward pass only, so there is nothing to train","D":"Only the new classifier head; every convolutional weight stays frozen","ans":"D","exp":"Feature extraction freezes every convolutional weight and trains only the new head, so it runs in minutes and needs very little data - at the cost of a fixed ceiling, because the features cannot adapt. \"All of them, with the...\" is fine-tuning. \"The convolutional backbone only; the...\" has the dial backwards: an untrained head on frozen features predicts nothing useful."},
  {"num":180,"week":8,"q":"A classifier is being built for four classes from folders of photographs. How many units should the output layer have, and which activation?","A":"4 units, ReLU","B":"2 units, softmax","C":"1 unit, sigmoid","D":"4 units, softmax","ans":"D","exp":"One unit per class, and softmax turns the four logits into probabilities that sum to 1, which is what a single-label four-way choice needs. \"1 unit, sigmoid\" is the binary setting. \"4 units, ReLU\" would leave the outputs unbounded and unnormalised, so they could not be read as probabilities."},
  {"num":181,"week":8,"q":"Which loss pairs with a softmax output layer for single-label multi-class classification?","A":"Huber loss","B":"Mean squared error","C":"Binary cross-entropy","D":"Categorical cross-entropy","ans":"D","exp":"Categorical cross-entropy reduces to -log(p of the true class), so it only looks at the probability assigned to the right answer. In PyTorch nn.CrossEntropyLoss() applies log-softmax itself, so softmax must not also be written into the model."},
  {"num":182,"week":8,"q":"What does a residual, or skip, connection do?","A":"It doubles the number of channels in every block","B":"It replaces pooling","C":"It removes the need for an activation function","D":"It adds the input of a block to its output, giving the gradient a short path backwards","ans":"D","exp":"The identity path means the gradient reaching an early layer is not forced through every intervening transformation, which is what made networks of 50 and 100 layers trainable at all."},
  {"num":183,"week":8,"q":"Global average pooling replaces flatten-then-dense at the end of a CNN. What is its main advantage?","A":"It increases the spatial resolution","B":"It makes the network invariant to colour","C":"It adds learnable parameters where they are most useful","D":"It collapses each channel to one number, removing the huge dense head and most of the parameter count","ans":"D","exp":"An 8x8x64 volume becomes 64 numbers with zero parameters, so a head that was half a million weights becomes a few hundred. It is also why the same network can then accept inputs of different sizes."},
  {"num":184,"week":8,"q":"When is fine-tuning the whole backbone the appropriate choice, rather than freezing it?","A":"Always, because more trainable parameters is always better","B":"Never; freezing is always safer","C":"When the new dataset is very small and very similar to the pre-training data","D":"When the new dataset is reasonably large, or its domain is far from the pre-training data","ans":"D","exp":"Frozen features only transfer when the new domain resembles the old one. X-rays or satellite imagery look nothing like ImageNet photographs, so the features have to be allowed to move - and that needs enough data to support it."},
  {"num":185,"week":8,"q":"Why is a pre-trained backbone usually fine-tuned with a much smaller learning rate than the head?","A":"Because the gradients reaching it are larger","B":"Because the head is frozen","C":"Because the backbone has more parameters","D":"Because its weights are already good, and large steps would destroy what was learned","ans":"D","exp":"The head starts random and needs big steps; the backbone starts from a well-trained solution and needs nudging. One global large rate would wreck the backbone in the first few batches, before the head has settled."},
  {"num":186,"week":8,"q":"A pre-trained ResNet-18 ends in a 512-feature vector. It is adapted to classify 5 classes by replacing the final layer. How many new parameters does that head have?","A":"5","B":"512","C":"2,560","D":"2,565","ans":"D","exp":"512 x 5 weights plus 5 biases = 2,560 + 5 = 2,565. The answer \"2,560\" forgets the biases. The point of the number is how small it is: that is the entire trainable parameter count in the feature-extraction setting."},
  {"num":187,"week":8,"q":"A pre-trained EfficientNet-B0 produces a 1,280-feature vector. Its 1,000-class classifier is replaced by a fresh dense layer for 8 classes, with one bias per class. How many parameters does the new head hold?","A":"8","B":"1,288","C":"10,240","D":"10,248","ans":"D","exp":"A dense layer from 1,280 inputs to 8 outputs holds 1,280 x 8 = 10,240 weights plus 8 biases, so 10,248. The answer \"10,240\" drops the bias term. The answer \"1,288\" adds 1,280 and 8 instead of multiplying them. The answer \"8\" counts only the biases."},
  {"num":188,"week":8,"q":"A CNN ends with a 7x7x512 feature map. Compare flatten-then-dense to 10 classes against global average pooling then dense to 10 classes, biases included. How many parameters does the pooling version save?","A":"5,130","B":"245,760","C":"250,890","D":"256,020","ans":"B","exp":"Flattening gives 7 x 7 x 512 = 25,088 inputs, so 250,890 parameters, while pooling gives 512 inputs, so 5,130; the saving is 245,760. The answer \"250,890\" is the flattened head itself, not the saving. The answer \"5,130\" is the pooled head itself. The answer \"256,020\" adds the two heads instead of subtracting."},
  {"num":189,"week":8,"q":"A ResNet-50 bottleneck block applies a 1x1 convolution from 256 to 64 channels, a 3x3 convolution from 64 to 64, then a 1x1 convolution from 64 back to 256. Ignoring biases, how many weights does it hold?","A":"36,864","B":"53,248","C":"69,632","D":"1,179,648","ans":"C","exp":"The three layers cost 256 x 64 = 16,384, then 9 x 64 x 64 = 36,864, then 64 x 256 = 16,384, giving 69,632. The answer \"36,864\" counts only the 3x3 layer. The answer \"53,248\" forgets one of the two 1x1 layers. The answer \"1,179,648\" is a plain pair of 3x3 convolutions kept at 256 channels."},
  {"num":190,"week":8,"q":"A ResNet-18 backbone holding 11,176,512 convolutional weights is frozen, and its 512-feature output feeds a fresh 7-class dense head with biases. How many parameters are trainable?","A":"7","B":"3,591","C":"11,176,512","D":"11,180,103","ans":"B","exp":"Only the head trains: 512 x 7 = 3,584 weights plus 7 biases, so 3,591. The answer \"11,180,103\" counts every parameter in the model, trainable or not. The answer \"11,176,512\" is the frozen backbone, which receives no updates. The answer \"7\" counts only the bias terms."},
  {"num":191,"week":8,"q":"A 1x1 bottleneck convolution squeezes a 384-channel feature map to 96 channels, with one bias per output channel. How many parameters does that layer hold?","A":"96","B":"480","C":"36,864","D":"36,960","ans":"D","exp":"A 1x1 kernel has no spatial extent, so the cost is 384 x 96 = 36,864 weights plus 96 biases, giving 36,960. The answer \"36,864\" drops the bias term. The answer \"480\" adds 384 and 96 instead of multiplying. The answer \"96\" counts only the biases."},
  {"num":192,"week":8,"q":"A pre-trained backbone is capped with a softmax classifier for a 12-class task. How many values does that output layer produce for one image?","A":"1","B":"12","C":"512","D":"1000","ans":"B","exp":"A softmax classification layer carries exactly one unit per class, so it emits 12 values for 12 classes. The answer \"1\" is the single-unit sigmoid output used for two-class problems. The answer \"512\" is the width of the feature vector feeding the head, not its output. The answer \"1000\" is the class count of the original ImageNet head that was removed."},
  {"num":193,"week":8,"q":"A layer maps a 14x14x256 feature map to 256 channels using a depthwise 3x3 convolution followed by a 1x1 pointwise convolution. Ignoring biases, how many weights does the separable pair hold?","A":"2,304","B":"65,536","C":"67,840","D":"589,824","ans":"C","exp":"Depthwise costs 9 x 256 = 2,304 and pointwise costs 256 x 256 = 65,536, giving 67,840 in total. The answer \"2,304\" counts only the depthwise stage. The answer \"65,536\" counts only the pointwise stage. The answer \"589,824\" is the standard 3x3 convolution the pair replaces."},
  {"num":194,"week":8,"q":"Four stacked residual blocks each have a residual-branch Jacobian magnitude dF/dx of 0.3. Using dL/dx = dL/dy x (1 + dF/dx) per block, by what factor is the gradient scaled across all four blocks?","A":"0.0081","B":"1.3","C":"2.8561","D":"5.2","ans":"C","exp":"Each block multiplies by 1 + 0.3 = 1.3, and four blocks give 1.3^4 = 2.8561. The answer \"0.0081\" is 0.3^4, the plain stack without shortcuts. The answer \"1.3\" is the factor for one block only. The answer \"5.2\" adds the four factors instead of multiplying them."},
  {"num":195,"week":8,"q":"In a residual block y = F(x) + x, why does gradient still reach x when the residual branch has learned almost nothing?","A":"Batch normalisation inside the block rescales the gradient back to magnitude one","B":"The shortcut re-scales dF/dx by a learned scalar that grows during training","C":"Differentiating the sum gives dL/dy x (1 + dF/dx), and the added 1 survives even when dF/dx collapses towards zero","D":"The block is skipped on the backward pass, so no derivative is needed at all","ans":"C","exp":"The identity path contributes a derivative of exactly 1, so the bracket (1 + dF/dx) cannot be driven to zero by a useless branch. The answer \"The block is skipped on the backward pass, so no derivative is needed at all\" is wrong because every layer in the block still runs and is still differentiated. The answer \"The shortcut re-scales dF/dx by a learned scalar that grows during training\" invents weights the shortcut does not have. The answer \"Batch normalisation inside the block rescales the gradient back to magnitude one\" describes activation scaling, not the identity term."},
  {"num":196,"week":8,"q":"What operation does global average pooling perform at the end of a convolutional network?","A":"It averages the channel axis at every position, leaving a single-channel map","B":"It averages the weights of the final convolution to shrink the model file","C":"It averages each final feature map over all its spatial positions, giving one number per channel","D":"It averages neighbouring pixels in 2x2 windows, halving the height and width","ans":"C","exp":"Global average pooling collapses each channel to a single number, turning an HxWxC map into a C-length vector that a small dense layer can classify. The answer \"It averages neighbouring pixels in 2x2 windows, halving the height and width\" is ordinary average pooling, which is local, not global. The answer \"It averages the channel axis at every position, leaving a single-channel map\" pools the wrong axis. The answer \"It averages the weights of the final convolution to shrink the model file\" confuses activations with weights."},
  {"num":197,"week":8,"q":"Compared with feature extraction, which weights are updated when the upper blocks of a pre-trained network are fine-tuned?","A":"Only the pre-trained convolutional weights; the new head is left at its random initial values","B":"Every convolutional weight in the network, always at the same rate as the head","C":"The new head plus the unfrozen upper blocks, with those blocks stepped at a much smaller rate","D":"Neither the head nor the backbone; only the batch-normalisation statistics move","ans":"C","exp":"Fine-tuning widens what moves: the head still trains, and the thawed upper blocks train as well but at roughly a hundredth of the head rate, while the lower blocks stay frozen. The answer \"Only the pre-trained convolutional weights; the new head is left at its random initial values\" would leave the classifier random. The answer \"Every convolutional weight in the network, always at the same rate as the head\" describes a full-network thaw at one rate, not partial fine-tuning. The answer \"Neither the head nor the backbone; only the batch-normalisation statistics move\" describes no training at all."},
  {"num":198,"week":8,"q":"In the transfer-learning workflow, why is the new head trained on its own before any backbone block is unfrozen?","A":"Because frozen convolutions cannot produce features until the head has converged","B":"Because the head must be trained on a different loss function from the backbone","C":"Because a randomly initialised head sends large gradients back and would wreck the pre-trained features in the first few batches","D":"Because the optimiser cannot hold two parameter groups at the same time","ans":"C","exp":"A fresh head starts random, so its early gradients are large; warming it first lets the backbone be thawed later without those gradients destroying good features. The answer \"Because frozen convolutions cannot produce features until the head has converged\" is wrong, as a frozen backbone produces features on the first forward pass. The answer \"Because the head must be trained on a different loss function from the backbone\" invents a second loss. The answer \"Because the optimiser cannot hold two parameter groups at the same time\" is false; two groups with different rates are exactly how stage two is written."},
  {"num":199,"week":8,"q":"What does the softmax output layer of a single-label image classifier produce, and what loss is it read by?","A":"One independent probability per class, each free to sum to more than one, read by binary cross-entropy","B":"A single scalar between 0 and 1 for the predicted class, read by Huber loss","C":"One non-negative value per class summing to one across classes, read by categorical cross-entropy","D":"One unbounded score per class, read by mean squared error against the one-hot target","ans":"C","exp":"Softmax normalises the layer scores into a distribution over the classes, and categorical cross-entropy scores the probability placed on the true class. The answer \"One unbounded score per class, read by mean squared error against the one-hot target\" describes raw logits with a regression loss. The answer \"One independent probability per class, each free to sum to more than one, read by binary cross-entropy\" is the multi-label sigmoid setting. The answer \"A single scalar between 0 and 1 for the predicted class, read by Huber loss\" collapses the output to one number."},
  {"num":200,"week":8,"q":"A pre-trained backbone is reused by replacing its classifier. What shape does the replacement head take?","A":"A copy of the original 1,000-class layer with the unused outputs set to zero","B":"A stack of convolutional layers matching the depth of the block below it","C":"A dense layer from the pooled feature vector to one unit per class of the new task","D":"A pooling layer alone, with no learnable weights at all","ans":"C","exp":"Only the final mapping changes: the pooled feature vector feeds a fresh dense layer whose width is the new class count, and that layer is the only randomly initialised part. The answer \"A copy of the original 1,000-class layer with the unused outputs set to zero\" keeps a head sized to the wrong task. The answer \"A stack of convolutional layers matching the depth of the block below it\" rebuilds a backbone rather than a head. The answer \"A pooling layer alone, with no learnable weights at all\" leaves nothing to map features to classes."}
];
